<\body>
  <notes-header>

  <chapter*|Papers>

  <notes-abstract|Preprints deposited here as an alternative to the usual
  archives (experimental).>

  Papers by Massimiliano Gubinelli and co-authors, every version kept with
  its sources and its PDF: the TeXmacs sources and an HTML version for the
  papers written with TeXmacs, the LaTeX sources and PDFs received by arXiv
  for the others. The archive follows the
  <hlink|OpenSheaf|https://opensheaf.org> format (version 0.1), where its
  structure and metadata are described; programs can start from its discovery
  document <hlink|opensheaf.json|opensheaf.json>.

  Each version keeps its own licence, given on the page of its paper: the
  licence under which arXiv distributes it, an open licence such as CC BY, or
  all rights reserved to the authors.

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Mass gap for the fractional <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^4_2>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^4_2\\)> model|paper/fractional-phi42-mass-gap/>>

    By Pawe\<#142\> Duch and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|HTML|site/fractional-phi42-mass-gap/fractional-phi42-mass-gap.html> \<cdot\> <hlink|PDF|ocfl/fractional-phi42-mass-gap/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <specific|texmacs|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/fractional-phi42-mass-gap/v1/content/fractional-phi42-mass-gap.tm>><specific|html*|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/fractional-phi42-mass-gap/v1/content/fractional-phi42-mass-gap.texmacs>> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2611.17119|https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17119>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We present a new, simple and self-contained technique for proving a mass gap for Euclidean quantum field theories in the high-temperature regime using the stochastic quantisation approach. As an application, we establish exponential decay of spatial correlations for the fractional <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^{4}>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^{4}\\)> model on the plane in the Wick-ordering regime. The key idea is to analyse the propagation of perturbations using the linearised stochastic quantisation equation. Spatial propagation is controlled by an auxiliary random walk, while the potentially destabilising effect of the renormalised nonlinearity is controlled by combining dissipative energy estimates for the nonlinear dynamic with the Boué\<#2013\>Dupuis variational formula.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We present a new, simple and self-contained technique for proving a mass gap for Euclidean quantum field theories in the high-temperature regime using the stochastic quantisation approach. As an application, we establish exponential decay of spatial correlations for the fractional <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^{4}>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^{4}\\)> model on the plane in the Wick-ordering regime. The key idea is to analyse the propagation of perturbations using the linearised stochastic quantisation equation. Spatial propagation is controlled by an auxiliary random walk, while the potentially destabilising effect of the renormalised nonlinearity is controlled by combining dissipative energy estimates for the nonlinear dynamic with the Boué\<#2013\>Dupuis variational formula.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 12 November 2026; version 1, 12 November 2026<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|b9879f5468b83168f6846cbfdcfd5b49...>|ocfl/fractional-phi42-mass-gap/v4/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Composite local observables of sine-Gordon via stochastic analysis|paper/sine-gordon-composite-observables/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli and Peter Paulovics.

    <hlink|HTML|site/sine-gordon-composite-observables/sine-gordon-composite-observables.html> \<cdot\> <hlink|PDF|ocfl/sine-gordon-composite-observables/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <specific|texmacs|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/sine-gordon-composite-observables/v1/content/sine-gordon-composite-observables.tm>><specific|html*|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/sine-gordon-composite-observables/v1/content/sine-gordon-composite-observables.texmacs>> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2611.14296|https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14296>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We develop a rigorous framework for the description of general local composite observables in Euclidean quantum field theories. The approach is nonperturbative and combines renormalisation group ideas with techniques of stochastic analysis. Via renormalisation dictated by sufficiently precise approximate solution theory of corresponding flow equations, we construct various local observables of the sine\<#2013\>Gordon model on the full space up to <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\beta^{2}<less>6\\pi>><specific|html*|\\(\\beta^{2}<less>6\\pi\\)>, including highly singular ones such as vertex fields <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|exp(i\\alpha\\phi(y))>><specific|html*|\\(exp(i\\alpha\\phi(y))\\)> in the optimal Wick regime <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\|\\Re e(\\alpha)\|<less>4\\pi/\\beta>><specific|html*|\\(\|\\Re e(\\alpha)\|<less>4\\pi/\\beta\\)> and derivatives of arbitrarily high order, i.e. <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\partial^{\\mu}\\phi(y)>><specific|html*|\\(\\partial^{\\mu}\\phi(y)\\)> for any multiindex <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mu\\in\\mathbb{N}^{2}_{0}>><specific|html*|\\(\\mu\\in\\mathbb{N}^{2}_{0}\\)>. Applications of the approach include a direct proof of smoothness of observable-inserted correlation functions, and verification that the constructed objects satisfy a version of the prefactorization algebra axioms adapted to our setting.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We develop a rigorous framework for the description of general local composite observables in Euclidean quantum field theories. The approach is nonperturbative and combines renormalisation group ideas with techniques of stochastic analysis. Via renormalisation dictated by sufficiently precise approximate solution theory of corresponding flow equations, we construct various local observables of the sine\<#2013\>Gordon model on the full space up to <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\beta^{2}<less>6\\pi>><specific|html*|\\(\\beta^{2}<less>6\\pi\\)>, including highly singular ones such as vertex fields <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|exp(i\\alpha\\phi(y))>><specific|html*|\\(exp(i\\alpha\\phi(y))\\)> in the optimal Wick regime <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\|\\Re e(\\alpha)\|<less>4\\pi/\\beta>><specific|html*|\\(\|\\Re e(\\alpha)\|<less>4\\pi/\\beta\\)> and derivatives of arbitrarily high order, i.e. <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\partial^{\\mu}\\phi(y)>><specific|html*|\\(\\partial^{\\mu}\\phi(y)\\)> for any multiindex <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mu\\in\\mathbb{N}^{2}_{0}>><specific|html*|\\(\\mu\\in\\mathbb{N}^{2}_{0}\\)>. Applications of the approach include a direct proof of smoothness of observable-inserted correlation functions, and verification that the constructed objects satisfy a version of the prefactorization algebra axioms adapted to our setting.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 10 November 2026; version 1, 12 November 2026<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|8699d71c97115dbc6ffd5cc07cad3118...>|ocfl/sine-gordon-composite-observables/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Nonlinear PDEs with modulated dispersion III: multiplicative noises|paper/modulated-dispersion-3/>>

    By Andreia Chapouto, Massimiliano Gubinelli, Guopeng Li, Jiawei Li and Tadahiro Oh.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-3/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-3/v1/content/source/SmodKdV_arXiv2.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2607.08385|https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08385>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We investigate pathwise well-posedness of the stochastic modulated Korteweg-de Vries equation (KdV) on the circle with a multiplicative noise, where a time non-homogeneous modulation acts on the linear dispersion term. (i) In the Young case (= fractional-in-time case with Hurst parameter greater than <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\frac 12>><specific|html*|\\(\\frac 12\\)>), we establish a new regularization-by-noise phenomenon on the stochastic convolution in a pathwise manner, where a gain of spatial regularity becomes (arbitrarily) larger for more irregular modulations. We then prove that, given any <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|s \\in \\mathbb R>><specific|html*|\\(s \\in \\mathbb R\\)> and any multiplicative Young noise, however rough it is in space, the stochastic modulated KdV is pathwise locally well-posed in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H^s(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(H^s(\\mathbb T)\\)>, provided that the modulation is sufficiently irregular. (ii) In the rough case (= white-in-time case), irregularity of the modulation does not induce any smoothing on the stochastic convolution, and in fact, there is a slight loss in the spatial regularity. In this case, by slightly regularizing the multiplicative noise term, we prove pathwise local well-posedness in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H^s(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(H^s(\\mathbb T)\\)> for any given <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|s \\in \\mathbb R>><specific|html*|\\(s \\in \\mathbb R\\)>, provided that the noise is sufficiently smooth in space. We achieve these goals by combining (i) the sewing lemma approach to the nonlinear Young integration theory, introduced by Chouk and the second author (2014), and (ii) the pathwise construction of stochastic convolutions as Young or rough integrals via the random tensor estimate and the sewing lemma, introduced by the first, fourth, and fifth authors (2026). In the appendix, we also present an example of regularization by noise for a stochastic modulated Schrödinger equation with a multiplicative Young noise.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We investigate pathwise well-posedness of the stochastic modulated Korteweg-de Vries equation (KdV) on the circle with a multiplicative noise, where a time non-homogeneous modulation acts on the linear dispersion term. (i) In the Young case (= fractional-in-time case with Hurst parameter greater than <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\frac 12>><specific|html*|\\(\\frac 12\\)>), we establish a new regularization-by-noise phenomenon on the stochastic convolution in a pathwise manner, where a gain of spatial regularity becomes (arbitrarily) larger for more irregular modulations. We then prove that, given any <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|s \\in \\mathbb R>><specific|html*|\\(s \\in \\mathbb R\\)> and any multiplicative Young noise, however rough it is in space, the stochastic modulated KdV is pathwise locally well-posed in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H^s(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(H^s(\\mathbb T)\\)>, provided that the modulation is sufficiently irregular. (ii) In the rough case (= white-in-time case), irregularity of the modulation does not induce any smoothing on the stochastic convolution, and in fact, there is a slight loss in the spatial regularity. In this case, by slightly regularizing the multiplicative noise term, we prove pathwise local well-posedness in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H^s(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(H^s(\\mathbb T)\\)> for any given <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|s \\in \\mathbb R>><specific|html*|\\(s \\in \\mathbb R\\)>, provided that the noise is sufficiently smooth in space. We achieve these goals by combining (i) the sewing lemma approach to the nonlinear Young integration theory, introduced by Chouk and the second author (2014), and (ii) the pathwise construction of stochastic convolutions as Young or rough integrals via the random tensor estimate and the sewing lemma, introduced by the first, fourth, and fifth authors (2026). In the appendix, we also present an example of regularization by noise for a stochastic modulated Schrödinger equation with a multiplicative Young noise.
    </html-div-class>>>

