  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Large field problem in coercive singular PDEs|paper/large-field-problem/>>

    By Ilya Chevyrev and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/large-field-problem/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/large-field-problem/v2/content/source/large_field.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2510.20716|https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20716>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We derive a priori estimates for singular differential equations of the form <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathcal{L} \\phi = P(\\phi,\\nabla\\phi) + f(\\phi,\\nabla\\phi)\\xi>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathcal{L} \\phi = P(\\phi,\\nabla\\phi) + f(\\phi,\\nabla\\phi)\\xi\\)> where <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|P>><specific|html*|\\(P\\)> is a polynomial, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|f>><specific|html*|\\(f\\)> is a sufficiently well-behaved function, and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\xi>><specific|html*|\\(\\xi\\)> is an irregular distribution such that the equation is subcritical. The differential operator <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathcal L>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathcal L\\)> is either a derivative in time, in which case we interpret the equation using rough path theory, or a heat operator, in which case we interpret the equation using regularity structures. Our only assumption on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|P>><specific|html*|\\(P\\)> is that solutions with <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\xi=0>><specific|html*|\\(\\xi=0\\)> exhibit coercivity. Our estimates are local in space and time, and independent of boundary conditions. One of our main results is an abstract estimate that allows one to pass from a local coercivity property to a global one using scaling, for a large class of equations. This allows us to reduce the problem of deriving a priori estimates to the case when <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\xi>><specific|html*|\\(\\xi\\)> is small.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We derive a priori estimates for singular differential equations of the form <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathcal{L} \\phi = P(\\phi,\\nabla\\phi) + f(\\phi,\\nabla\\phi)\\xi>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathcal{L} \\phi = P(\\phi,\\nabla\\phi) + f(\\phi,\\nabla\\phi)\\xi\\)> where <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|P>><specific|html*|\\(P\\)> is a polynomial, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|f>><specific|html*|\\(f\\)> is a sufficiently well-behaved function, and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\xi>><specific|html*|\\(\\xi\\)> is an irregular distribution such that the equation is subcritical. The differential operator <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathcal L>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathcal L\\)> is either a derivative in time, in which case we interpret the equation using rough path theory, or a heat operator, in which case we interpret the equation using regularity structures. Our only assumption on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|P>><specific|html*|\\(P\\)> is that solutions with <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\xi=0>><specific|html*|\\(\\xi=0\\)> exhibit coercivity. Our estimates are local in space and time, and independent of boundary conditions. One of our main results is an abstract estimate that allows one to pass from a local coercivity property to a global one using scaling, for a large class of equations. This allows us to reduce the problem of deriving a priori estimates to the case when <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\xi>><specific|html*|\\(\\xi\\)> is small.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 23 October 2025; version 2 (arXiv v2), 12 December 2025<next-line>arXiv comments: 71 pages, 3 figures, minor changes<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|a005d0975cf6e0559916f562775e9f2c...>|ocfl/large-field-problem/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Nonlinear PDEs with modulated dispersion IV: normal form approach and unconditional uniqueness|paper/modulated-dispersion-4/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli, Guopeng Li, Jiawei Li and Tadahiro Oh.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-4/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-4/v2/content/source/KdVNF_accepted_arXiv.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2505.24270|https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.24270>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We study the modulated Korteweg\<#2013\>de Vries equation (KdV) on the circle with a time non-homogeneous modulation acting on the linear dispersion term. By adapting the normal form approach to the modulated setting, we prove sharp unconditional uniqueness of solutions to the modulated KdV in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^2(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(L^2(\\mathbb T)\\)> if a modulation is sufficiently irregular. For example, this result implies that if the modulation is given by a sample path of a fractional Brownian motion with Hurst index <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|0 <less> H <less> \\frac 25>><specific|html*|\\(0 <less> H <less> \\frac 25\\)>, the modulated KdV on the circle is unconditionally well-posed in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^2(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(L^2(\\mathbb T)\\)>. Our normal form approach provides the construction of solutions to the modulated KdV (and the associated nonlinear Young integral) without assuming any positive regularity in time. As an interesting byproduct of our normal form approach, we extend the construction of the nonlinear Young integral to a much larger class of functions, and obtain an improved Euler approximation scheme as compared to the classical sewing lemma approach. We also establish analogous sharp unconditional uniqueness results for the modulated Benjamin-Ono equation and the modulated derivative nonlinear Schrödinger equation (NLS) with a quadratic nonlinearity. In the appendix, we prove sharp unconditional uniqueness of the cubic modulated NLS on the circle in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H^{\\frac 16}(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(H^{\\frac 16}(\\mathbb T)\\)>.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We study the modulated Korteweg\<#2013\>de Vries equation (KdV) on the circle with a time non-homogeneous modulation acting on the linear dispersion term. By adapting the normal form approach to the modulated setting, we prove sharp unconditional uniqueness of solutions to the modulated KdV in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^2(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(L^2(\\mathbb T)\\)> if a modulation is sufficiently irregular. For example, this result implies that if the modulation is given by a sample path of a fractional Brownian motion with Hurst index <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|0 <less> H <less> \\frac 25>><specific|html*|\\(0 <less> H <less> \\frac 25\\)>, the modulated KdV on the circle is unconditionally well-posed in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^2(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(L^2(\\mathbb T)\\)>. Our normal form approach provides the construction of solutions to the modulated KdV (and the associated nonlinear Young integral) without assuming any positive regularity in time. As an interesting byproduct of our normal form approach, we extend the construction of the nonlinear Young integral to a much larger class of functions, and obtain an improved Euler approximation scheme as compared to the classical sewing lemma approach. We also establish analogous sharp unconditional uniqueness results for the modulated Benjamin-Ono equation and the modulated derivative nonlinear Schrödinger equation (NLS) with a quadratic nonlinearity. In the appendix, we prove sharp unconditional uniqueness of the cubic modulated NLS on the circle in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H^{\\frac 16}(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(H^{\\frac 16}(\\mathbb T)\\)>.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 30 May 2025; version 2 (arXiv v2), 24 February 2026<next-line>arXiv comments: 40 pages. Minor revision. To appear in Peking Math. J<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|ae0150a43dbd2caa23f6108417147229...>|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-4/v3/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|A simple construction of the sine-Gordon model via stochastic quantization|paper/sine-gordon-simple-construction/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli, Martin Hairer, Tadahiro Oh and Younes Zine.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/sine-gordon-simple-construction/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/sine-gordon-simple-construction/v1/content/source/2412.16404.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2412.16404|https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.16404>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We present a simple PDE construction of the sine-Gordon measure below the first threshold (<specific|texmacs|<verbatim|{\\beta}^2 <less> 4\\pi>><specific|html*|\\({\\beta}^2 <less> 4\\pi\\)>), in both the finite and infinite volume settings, by studying the corresponding parabolic sine-Gordon model. We also establish pathwise global well-posedness of the hyperbolic sine-Gordon model in finite volume for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|{\\beta}^2 <less> 2\\pi>><specific|html*|\\({\\beta}^2 <less> 2\\pi\\)>.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We present a simple PDE construction of the sine-Gordon measure below the first threshold (<specific|texmacs|<verbatim|{\\beta}^2 <less> 4\\pi>><specific|html*|\\({\\beta}^2 <less> 4\\pi\\)>), in both the finite and infinite volume settings, by studying the corresponding parabolic sine-Gordon model. We also establish pathwise global well-posedness of the hyperbolic sine-Gordon model in finite volume for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|{\\beta}^2 <less> 2\\pi>><specific|html*|\\({\\beta}^2 <less> 2\\pi\\)>.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 20 December 2024; version 1 (arXiv v1), 20 December 2024<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|e7deee9499f0617e92139b4ecbd93dc3...>|ocfl/sine-gordon-simple-construction/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|The FBSDE approach to sine-Gordon up to <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|6\\pi>><specific|html*|\\(6\\pi\\)>|paper/sine-gordon-fbsde/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli and Sarah-Jean Meyer.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/sine-gordon-fbsde/v3/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/sine-gordon-fbsde/v3/content/source/main.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2401.13648|https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.13648>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We develop a stochastic analysis of the sine-Gordon Euclidean quantum field <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|(\\cos (\\beta \\varphi))_2>><specific|html*|\\((\\cos (\\beta \\varphi))_2\\)> on the full space up to the second threshold, i.e. for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\beta^2 <less> 6 \\pi>><specific|html*|\\(\\beta^2 <less> 6 \\pi\\)>. The basis of our method is a forward-backward stochastic differential equation (FBSDE) for a decomposition <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|(X_t)_{t \\geqslant 0}>><specific|html*|\\((X_t)_{t \\geqslant 0}\\)> of the interacting Euclidean field <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|X_{\\infty}>><specific|html*|\\(X_{\\infty}\\)> along a scale parameter <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|t \\geqslant 0>><specific|html*|\\(t \\geqslant 0\\)>. This FBSDE describes the optimiser of the stochastic control representation of the Euclidean QFT introduced by Barashkov and one of the authors. We show that the FBSDE provides a description of the interacting field without cut-offs and that it can be used effectively to study the sine-Gordon measure to obtain results about large deviations, integrability, decay of correlations for local observables, singularity with respect to the free field, Osterwalder-Schrader axioms and other properties.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We develop a stochastic analysis of the sine-Gordon Euclidean quantum field <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|(\\cos (\\beta \\varphi))_2>><specific|html*|\\((\\cos (\\beta \\varphi))_2\\)> on the full space up to the second threshold, i.e. for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\beta^2 <less> 6 \\pi>><specific|html*|\\(\\beta^2 <less> 6 \\pi\\)>. The basis of our method is a forward-backward stochastic differential equation (FBSDE) for a decomposition <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|(X_t)_{t \\geqslant 0}>><specific|html*|\\((X_t)_{t \\geqslant 0}\\)> of the interacting Euclidean field <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|X_{\\infty}>><specific|html*|\\(X_{\\infty}\\)> along a scale parameter <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|t \\geqslant 0>><specific|html*|\\(t \\geqslant 0\\)>. This FBSDE describes the optimiser of the stochastic control representation of the Euclidean QFT introduced by Barashkov and one of the authors. We show that the FBSDE provides a description of the interacting field without cut-offs and that it can be used effectively to study the sine-Gordon measure to obtain results about large deviations, integrability, decay of correlations for local observables, singularity with respect to the free field, Osterwalder-Schrader axioms and other properties.
    </html-div-class>>>

