  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Gaussian Fluctuations for the stochastic Burgers equation in dimension <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d\\geq 2>><specific|html*|\\(d\\geq 2\\)>|paper/burgers-gaussian-fluctuations/>>

    By Giuseppe Cannizzaro, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Fabio Toninelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/burgers-gaussian-fluctuations/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/burgers-gaussian-fluctuations/v2/content/source/ASEPSPDENEW.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2304.05730|https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.05730>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        The goal of the present paper is to establish a framework which allows to rigorously determine the large-scale Gaussian fluctuations for a class of singular SPDEs at and above criticality, and therefore beyond the range of applicability of pathwise techniques, such as the theory of Regularity Structures. To this purpose, we focus on a <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d>><specific|html*|\\(d\\)>-dimensional generalization of the Stochastic Burgers equation (SBE) introduced in [H. van Beijeren, R. Kutner and H. Spohn, Excess noise for driven diffusive systems, PRL, 1985]. In both the critical <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d=2>><specific|html*|\\(d=2\\)> and super-critical <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d\\geq 3>><specific|html*|\\(d\\geq 3\\)> cases, we show that the scaling limit of (the regularised) SBE is given by a stochastic heat equation with non-trivially renormalised coefficient, introducing a set of tools that we expect to be applicable more widely. For <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d\\ge3>><specific|html*|\\(d\\ge3\\)> the scaling adopted is the classical diffusive one, while in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d=2>><specific|html*|\\(d=2\\)> it is the weak coupling scaling which corresponds to tuning down the strength of the interaction in a scale-dependent way.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        The goal of the present paper is to establish a framework which allows to rigorously determine the large-scale Gaussian fluctuations for a class of singular SPDEs at and above criticality, and therefore beyond the range of applicability of pathwise techniques, such as the theory of Regularity Structures. To this purpose, we focus on a <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d>><specific|html*|\\(d\\)>-dimensional generalization of the Stochastic Burgers equation (SBE) introduced in [H. van Beijeren, R. Kutner and H. Spohn, Excess noise for driven diffusive systems, PRL, 1985]. In both the critical <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d=2>><specific|html*|\\(d=2\\)> and super-critical <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d\\geq 3>><specific|html*|\\(d\\geq 3\\)> cases, we show that the scaling limit of (the regularised) SBE is given by a stochastic heat equation with non-trivially renormalised coefficient, introducing a set of tools that we expect to be applicable more widely. For <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d\\ge3>><specific|html*|\\(d\\ge3\\)> the scaling adopted is the classical diffusive one, while in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d=2>><specific|html*|\\(d=2\\)> it is the weak coupling scaling which corresponds to tuning down the strength of the interaction in a scale-dependent way.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 12 April 2023; version 2 (arXiv v2), 16 October 2023<next-line>arXiv comments: 68 pages, 4 figures. A mistake in an earlier version has been corrected<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|12b3c533f5f2b42f873f56161543a152...>|ocfl/burgers-gaussian-fluctuations/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Parabolic stochastic quantisation of the fractional <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^4_3>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^4_3\\)> model in the full subcritical regime|paper/fractional-phi43/>>

    By Pawe\<#142\> Duch, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Paolo Rinaldi.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/fractional-phi43/v5/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/fractional-phi43/v5/content/source/fractional-phi43-flow-jems.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2303.18112|https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.18112>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We present a construction of the measure of the fractional <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^4>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^4\\)> Euclidean quantum field theory on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^3>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^3\\)> in the full subcritical regime via parabolic stochastic quantisation. Our approach is based on a truncated flow equation for the effective description of the model at sufficiently small scales and on coercive estimates for the nonlinear stochastic partial differential equation describing the interacting field. The constructed measure is translation invariant, reflection positive, and has quartic exponential tails.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We present a construction of the measure of the fractional <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^4>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^4\\)> Euclidean quantum field theory on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^3>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^3\\)> in the full subcritical regime via parabolic stochastic quantisation. Our approach is based on a truncated flow equation for the effective description of the model at sufficiently small scales and on coercive estimates for the nonlinear stochastic partial differential equation describing the interacting field. The constructed measure is translation invariant, reflection positive, and has quartic exponential tails.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 31 March 2023; version 5 (arXiv v5), 12 November 2026<next-line>arXiv comments: 136 pages, published version<next-line>pin:
