  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|A singular integration by parts formula for the exponential Euclidean QFT on the plane|paper/exponential-model-ibp/>>

    By Francesco C. De Vecchi, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Mattia Turra.

    <hlink|HTML|site/exponential-model-ibp/exponential-model-ibp.html> \<cdot\> <hlink|PDF|ocfl/exponential-model-ibp/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <specific|texmacs|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/exponential-model-ibp/v1/content/source/exp-model-30.tm>><specific|html*|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/exponential-model-ibp/v1/content/source/exp-model-30.texmacs>> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2212.05584|https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.05584>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We give a novel characterization of the Euclidean quantum field theory with exponential interaction <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\nu>><specific|html*|\\(\\nu\\)> on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^2>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^2\\)> through a renormalized integration by parts (IbP) formula, or otherwise said via an Euclidean Dyson-Schwinger equation for expected values of observables. In order to obtain the well-posedness of the singular IbP problem, we import some ideas used to analyse singular SPDEs and we require the measure to "look like" the Gaussian free field (GFF) in the sense that a suitable Wasserstein distance from the GFF is finite. This guarantees the existence of a nice coupling with the GFF which allows to control the renormalized IbP formula.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We give a novel characterization of the Euclidean quantum field theory with exponential interaction <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\nu>><specific|html*|\\(\\nu\\)> on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^2>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^2\\)> through a renormalized integration by parts (IbP) formula, or otherwise said via an Euclidean Dyson-Schwinger equation for expected values of observables. In order to obtain the well-posedness of the singular IbP problem, we import some ideas used to analyse singular SPDEs and we require the measure to "look like" the Gaussian free field (GFF) in the sense that a suitable Wasserstein distance from the GFF is finite. This guarantees the existence of a nice coupling with the GFF which allows to control the renormalized IbP formula.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 11 December 2022; version 1 (arXiv v1), 11 December 2022<next-line>arXiv comments: 78 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|1f1008793eaac7df2e2ff37ce6d52eea...>|ocfl/exponential-model-ibp/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|A stochastic analysis of subcritical Euclidean fermionic field theories|paper/fermionic-field-theories/>>

    By Francesco C. De Vecchi, Luca Fresta and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|HTML|site/fermionic-field-theories/fermionic-field-theories.html> \<cdot\> <hlink|PDF|ocfl/fermionic-field-theories/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <specific|texmacs|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/fermionic-field-theories/v2/content/source/fermionic-flow-v92-black.tm>><specific|html*|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/fermionic-field-theories/v2/content/source/fermionic-flow-v92-black.texmacs>> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2210.15047|https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.15047>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        Building on previous work on the stochastic analysis for Grassmann random variables, we introduce a forward-backward stochastic differential equation (FBSDE) which provides a stochastic quantisation of Grassmann measures. Our method is inspired by the so-called continuous renormalisation group, but avoids the technical difficulties encountered in the direct study of the flow equation for the effective potentials. As an application, we construct a family of weakly coupled subcritical Euclidean fermionic field theories and prove exponential decay of correlations.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        Building on previous work on the stochastic analysis for Grassmann random variables, we introduce a forward-backward stochastic differential equation (FBSDE) which provides a stochastic quantisation of Grassmann measures. Our method is inspired by the so-called continuous renormalisation group, but avoids the technical difficulties encountered in the direct study of the flow equation for the effective potentials. As an application, we construct a family of weakly coupled subcritical Euclidean fermionic field theories and prove exponential decay of correlations.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 26 October 2022; version 2 (arXiv v2), 20 June 2024<next-line>arXiv comments: The errors in some proofs have been corrected and some typos have been fixed. Accepted for publication in Annals of Probability<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|abb1020de790d83d98311b2121baf0b9...>|ocfl/fermionic-field-theories/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|On the variational method for Euclidean quantum fields in infinite volume|paper/variational-infinite-volume/>>

    By Nikolay Barashkov and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|HTML|site/variational-infinite-volume/variational-infinite-volume.html> \<cdot\> <hlink|PDF|ocfl/variational-infinite-volume/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <specific|texmacs|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/variational-infinite-volume/v2/content/source/thermo-limit-equations-31-revision-1-arxiv.tm>><specific|html*|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/variational-infinite-volume/v2/content/source/thermo-limit-equations-31-revision-1-arxiv.texmacs>> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2112.05562|https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.05562>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We investigate the infinite volume limit of the variational description of Euclidean quantum fields introduced in a previous work. Focussing on two dimensional theories for simplicity, we prove in details how to use the variational approach to obtain tightness of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\varphi^4_2>><specific|html*|\\(\\varphi^4_2\\)> without cutoffs and a corresponding large deviation principle for any infinite volume limit. Any infinite volume measure is described via a forward\<#2013\>backwards stochastic differential equation in weak form (wFBSDE). Similar considerations apply to more general <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|P (\\varphi)_2>><specific|html*|\\(P (\\varphi)_2\\)> theories. We consider also the <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\exp (\\beta \\varphi)_2>><specific|html*|\\(\\exp (\\beta \\varphi)_2\\)> model for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\beta^2 <less> 8 \\pi>><specific|html*|\\(\\beta^2 <less> 8 \\pi\\)> (the so called full <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^1>><specific|html*|\\(L^1\\)> regime) and prove uniqueness of the infinite volume limit and a variational characterization of the unique infinite volume measure. The corresponding characterization for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|P (\\varphi)_2>><specific|html*|\\(P (\\varphi)_2\\)> theories is lacking due to the difficulty of studying the stability of the wFBSDE against local perturbations.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We investigate the infinite volume limit of the variational description of Euclidean quantum fields introduced in a previous work. Focussing on two dimensional theories for simplicity, we prove in details how to use the variational approach to obtain tightness of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\varphi^4_2>><specific|html*|\\(\\varphi^4_2\\)> without cutoffs and a corresponding large deviation principle for any infinite volume limit. Any infinite volume measure is described via a forward\<#2013\>backwards stochastic differential equation in weak form (wFBSDE). Similar considerations apply to more general <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|P (\\varphi)_2>><specific|html*|\\(P (\\varphi)_2\\)> theories. We consider also the <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\exp (\\beta \\varphi)_2>><specific|html*|\\(\\exp (\\beta \\varphi)_2\\)> model for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\beta^2 <less> 8 \\pi>><specific|html*|\\(\\beta^2 <less> 8 \\pi\\)> (the so called full <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^1>><specific|html*|\\(L^1\\)> regime) and prove uniqueness of the infinite volume limit and a variational characterization of the unique infinite volume measure. The corresponding characterization for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|P (\\varphi)_2>><specific|html*|\\(P (\\varphi)_2\\)> theories is lacking due to the difficulty of studying the stability of the wFBSDE against local perturbations.
    </html-div-class>>>

