    <hlink|<verbatim|2507e42a7ca8a841d7ead096d6da81e0...>|ocfl/variational-infinite-volume/v3/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Mixing for generic rough shear flows|paper/rough-shear-flows-mixing/>>

    By Lucio Galeati and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/rough-shear-flows-mixing/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/rough-shear-flows-mixing/v2/content/source/2107.12115.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2107.12115|https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.12115>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We study mixing and diffusion properties of passive scalars driven by <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|generic>><specific|html*|\\(generic\\)> rough shear flows. Genericity is here understood in the sense of prevalence and (ir)regularity is measured in the Besov-Nikolskii scale <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|B^{\\alpha}_{1, \\infty}>><specific|html*|\\(B^{\\alpha}_{1, \\infty}\\)>, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha \\in (0, 1)>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha \\in (0, 1)\\)>. We provide upper and lower bounds, showing that in general inviscid mixing in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H^{1/2}>><specific|html*|\\(H^{1/2}\\)> holds sharply with rate <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|r(t) \\sim t^{1/(2 \\alpha)}>><specific|html*|\\(r(t) \\sim t^{1/(2 \\alpha)}\\)>, while enhanced dissipation holds with rate <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|r(\\nu) \\sim \\nu^{\\alpha / (\\alpha+2)}>><specific|html*|\\(r(\\nu) \\sim \\nu^{\\alpha / (\\alpha+2)}\\)>. Our results in the inviscid mixing case rely on the concept of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregularity, first introduced by Catellier and Gubinelli (Stoc. Proc. Appl. 126, 2016) and provide some new insights compared to the behavior predicted by Colombo, Coti Zelati and Widmayer (arXiv:2009.12268, 2020).
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We study mixing and diffusion properties of passive scalars driven by <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|generic>><specific|html*|\\(generic\\)> rough shear flows. Genericity is here understood in the sense of prevalence and (ir)regularity is measured in the Besov-Nikolskii scale <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|B^{\\alpha}_{1, \\infty}>><specific|html*|\\(B^{\\alpha}_{1, \\infty}\\)>, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha \\in (0, 1)>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha \\in (0, 1)\\)>. We provide upper and lower bounds, showing that in general inviscid mixing in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H^{1/2}>><specific|html*|\\(H^{1/2}\\)> holds sharply with rate <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|r(t) \\sim t^{1/(2 \\alpha)}>><specific|html*|\\(r(t) \\sim t^{1/(2 \\alpha)}\\)>, while enhanced dissipation holds with rate <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|r(\\nu) \\sim \\nu^{\\alpha / (\\alpha+2)}>><specific|html*|\\(r(\\nu) \\sim \\nu^{\\alpha / (\\alpha+2)}\\)>. Our results in the inviscid mixing case rely on the concept of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregularity, first introduced by Catellier and Gubinelli (Stoc. Proc. Appl. 126, 2016) and provide some new insights compared to the behavior predicted by Colombo, Coti Zelati and Widmayer (arXiv:2009.12268, 2020).
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 26 July 2021; version 2 (arXiv v2), 21 June 2023<next-line>arXiv comments: 32 pages, final accepted version<next-line>pin:
