  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Prevalence of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregularity and related properties|paper/rho-irregularity-prevalence/>>

    By Lucio Galeati and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/rho-irregularity-prevalence/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/rho-irregularity-prevalence/v2/content/source/rho-irregularity-revision-R2.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2004.00872|https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.00872>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We show that generic Hölder continuous functions are <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregular. The property of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregularity has been first introduced by Catellier and Gubinelli (Stoc. Proc. Appl. 126, 2016) and plays a key role in the study of well-posedness for some classes of perturbed ODEs and PDEs. Genericity here is understood in the sense of prevalence. As a consequence we obtain several results on regularisation by noise "without probability", i.e. without committing to specific assumptions on the statistical properties of the perturbations. We also establish useful criteria for stochastic processes to be <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregular and study in detail the geometric and analytic properties of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregular functions.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We show that generic Hölder continuous functions are <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregular. The property of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregularity has been first introduced by Catellier and Gubinelli (Stoc. Proc. Appl. 126, 2016) and plays a key role in the study of well-posedness for some classes of perturbed ODEs and PDEs. Genericity here is understood in the sense of prevalence. As a consequence we obtain several results on regularisation by noise "without probability", i.e. without committing to specific assumptions on the statistical properties of the perturbations. We also establish useful criteria for stochastic processes to be <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregular and study in detail the geometric and analytic properties of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregular functions.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 2 April 2020; version 2 (arXiv v2), 20 April 2023<next-line>arXiv comments: Final accepted version<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|4c9090dacc00f6f59d16b8364c55585e...>|ocfl/rho-irregularity-prevalence/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Noiseless regularisation by noise|paper/noiseless-regularisation/>>

    By Lucio Galeati and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|HTML|site/noiseless-regularisation/noiseless-regularisation.html> \<cdot\> <hlink|PDF|ocfl/noiseless-regularisation/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <specific|texmacs|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/noiseless-regularisation/v2/content/texmacs/prevalence-ode-revised.tm>><specific|html*|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/noiseless-regularisation/v2/content/texmacs/prevalence-ode-revised.texmacs>> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:2003.14264|https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.14264>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We analyse the effect of a generic continuous additive perturbation to the well-posedness of ordinary differential equations. Genericity here is understood in the sense of prevalence. This allows us to discuss these problems in a setting where we do not have to commit ourselves to any restrictive assumption on the statistical properties of the perturbation. The main result is that a generic continuous perturbation renders the Cauchy problem well-posed for arbitrarily irregular vector fields. Therefore we establish regularisation by noise "without probability".
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We analyse the effect of a generic continuous additive perturbation to the well-posedness of ordinary differential equations. Genericity here is understood in the sense of prevalence. This allows us to discuss these problems in a setting where we do not have to commit ourselves to any restrictive assumption on the statistical properties of the perturbation. The main result is that a generic continuous perturbation renders the Cauchy problem well-posed for arbitrarily irregular vector fields. Therefore we establish regularisation by noise "without probability".
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 31 March 2020; version 2 (arXiv v2), 12 December 2020<next-line>arXiv comments: Remark 21 and Section 5.2 revisited; Remark 11 added; typos corrected, references added<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|fcbf26b8db0b99518b5574c26a2db1ec...>|ocfl/noiseless-regularisation/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Hyperviscous stochastic Navier-Stokes equations with white noise invariant measure in two dimensions|paper/hyperviscous-navier-stokes/>>

    By M. Gubinelli and M. Turra.

    <hlink|HTML|site/hyperviscous-navier-stokes/hyperviscous-navier-stokes.html> \<cdot\> <hlink|PDF|ocfl/hyperviscous-navier-stokes/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <specific|texmacs|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/hyperviscous-navier-stokes/v2/content/texmacs/NSGen-fullspace-v06.tm>><specific|html*|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/hyperviscous-navier-stokes/v2/content/texmacs/NSGen-fullspace-v06.texmacs>> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1912.06881|https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.06881>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We prove existence and uniqueness of martingale solutions to a (slightly) hyperviscous stochastic Navier-Stokes equation in 2d with initial conditions absolutely continuous with respect to the Gibbs measure associated to the energy, getting the results both in the torus and in the whole space setting.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We prove existence and uniqueness of martingale solutions to a (slightly) hyperviscous stochastic Navier-Stokes equation in 2d with initial conditions absolutely continuous with respect to the Gibbs measure associated to the energy, getting the results both in the torus and in the whole space setting.
    </html-div-class>>>

