  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|The elliptic stochastic quantization of some two dimensional Euclidean QFTs|paper/elliptic-quantisation-2d/>>

    By Sergio Albeverio, Francesco C. De Vecchi and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/elliptic-quantisation-2d/v4/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/elliptic-quantisation-2d/v4/content/source/multi-elliptic-stochastic-quant-rev2-def.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1906.11187|https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.11187>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We study a class of elliptic SPDEs with additive Gaussian noise on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^2 \\times M>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^2 \\times M\\)>, with <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|M>><specific|html*|\\(M\\)> a <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d>><specific|html*|\\(d\\)>-dimensional manifold equipped with a positive Radon measure, and a real-valued non linearity given by the derivative of a smooth potential <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|V>><specific|html*|\\(V\\)>, convex at infinity and growing at most exponentially. For quite general coefficients and a suitable regularity of the noise we obtain, via the dimensional reduction principle discussed in our previous paper on the topic, the identity between the law of the solution to the SPDE evaluated at the origin with a Gibbs type measure on the abstract Wiener space <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^2 (M)>><specific|html*|\\(L^2 (M)\\)>. The results are then applied to the elliptic stochastic quantization equation for the scalar field with polynomial interaction over <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{T}^2>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{T}^2\\)>, and with exponential interaction over <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^2>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^2\\)> (known also as Høeg-Krohn or Liouville model in the literature). In particular for the exponential interaction case, the existence and uniqueness properties of solutions to the elliptic equation over <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^{2 + 2}>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^{2 + 2}\\)> is derived as well as the dimensional reduction for the values of the \<#201C\>charge parameter\<#201D\> <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\sigma = \\frac{\\alpha}{2\\sqrt{\\pi}} <less> \\sqrt{4 \\left( 8 - 4 \\sqrt{3} \\right) \\pi} \\simeq \\sqrt{4.23\\pi}>><specific|html*|\\(\\sigma = \\frac{\\alpha}{2\\sqrt{\\pi}} <less> \\sqrt{4 \\left( 8 - 4 \\sqrt{3} \\right) \\pi} \\simeq \\sqrt{4.23\\pi}\\)>, for which the model has an Euclidean invariant probability measure (hence also permitting to get the corresponding relativistic invariant model on the two dimensional Minkowski space).
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We study a class of elliptic SPDEs with additive Gaussian noise on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^2 \\times M>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^2 \\times M\\)>, with <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|M>><specific|html*|\\(M\\)> a <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d>><specific|html*|\\(d\\)>-dimensional manifold equipped with a positive Radon measure, and a real-valued non linearity given by the derivative of a smooth potential <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|V>><specific|html*|\\(V\\)>, convex at infinity and growing at most exponentially. For quite general coefficients and a suitable regularity of the noise we obtain, via the dimensional reduction principle discussed in our previous paper on the topic, the identity between the law of the solution to the SPDE evaluated at the origin with a Gibbs type measure on the abstract Wiener space <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^2 (M)>><specific|html*|\\(L^2 (M)\\)>. The results are then applied to the elliptic stochastic quantization equation for the scalar field with polynomial interaction over <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{T}^2>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{T}^2\\)>, and with exponential interaction over <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^2>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^2\\)> (known also as Høeg-Krohn or Liouville model in the literature). In particular for the exponential interaction case, the existence and uniqueness properties of solutions to the elliptic equation over <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^{2 + 2}>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^{2 + 2}\\)> is derived as well as the dimensional reduction for the values of the \<#201C\>charge parameter\<#201D\> <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\sigma = \\frac{\\alpha}{2\\sqrt{\\pi}} <less> \\sqrt{4 \\left( 8 - 4 \\sqrt{3} \\right) \\pi} \\simeq \\sqrt{4.23\\pi}>><specific|html*|\\(\\sigma = \\frac{\\alpha}{2\\sqrt{\\pi}} <less> \\sqrt{4 \\left( 8 - 4 \\sqrt{3} \\right) \\pi} \\simeq \\sqrt{4.23\\pi}\\)>, for which the model has an Euclidean invariant probability measure (hence also permitting to get the corresponding relativistic invariant model on the two dimensional Minkowski space).
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 26 June 2019; version 4 (arXiv v4), 31 July 2020<next-line>arXiv comments: There was an important error in Lemma 26 of the previous version which has been fixed in the present one. In order to correct the error, the interval of admissible "charge parameters" has been reduced<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|5c94b3f8c55e7f8b647b51d36ff6132b...>|ocfl/elliptic-quantisation-2d/v4/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Elliptic stochastic quantization|paper/elliptic-stochastic-quantisation/>>

    By Sergio Albeverio, Francesco C. De Vecchi and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|HTML|site/elliptic-stochastic-quantisation/elliptic-stochastic-quantisation.html> \<cdot\> <hlink|PDF|ocfl/elliptic-stochastic-quantisation/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <specific|texmacs|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/elliptic-stochastic-quantisation/v2/content/texmacs/elliptic-stochastic-quant-19-fra.tm>><specific|html*|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/elliptic-stochastic-quantisation/v2/content/texmacs/elliptic-stochastic-quant-19-fra.texmacs>> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1812.04422|https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.04422>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We prove an explicit formula for the law in zero of the solution of a class of elliptic SPDE in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^2>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^2\\)>. This formula is the simplest instance of dimensional reduction, discovered in the physics literature by Parisi and Sourlas (1979), which links the law of an elliptic SPDE in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d + 2>><specific|html*|\\(d + 2\\)> dimension with a Gibbs measure in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d>><specific|html*|\\(d\\)> dimensions. This phenomenon is similar to the relation between an <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^{d + 1}>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^{d + 1}\\)> dimensional parabolic SPDE and its <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^d>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^d\\)> dimensional invariant measure. As such, dimensional reduction of elliptic SPDEs can be considered a sort of elliptic stochastic quantisation procedure in the sense of Nelson (1966) and Parisi and Wu (1981). Our proof uses in a fundamental way the representation of the law of the SPDE as a supersymmetric quantum field theory. Dimensional reduction for the supersymmetric theory was already established by Klein et al. (1984). We fix a subtle gap in their proof and also complete the dimensional reduction picture by providing the link between the elliptic SPDE and the supersymmetric model. Even in our <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d = 0>><specific|html*|\\(d = 0\\)> context the arguments are non-trivial and a non-supersymmetric, elementary proof seems only to be available in the Gaussian case.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We prove an explicit formula for the law in zero of the solution of a class of elliptic SPDE in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^2>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^2\\)>. This formula is the simplest instance of dimensional reduction, discovered in the physics literature by Parisi and Sourlas (1979), which links the law of an elliptic SPDE in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d + 2>><specific|html*|\\(d + 2\\)> dimension with a Gibbs measure in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d>><specific|html*|\\(d\\)> dimensions. This phenomenon is similar to the relation between an <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^{d + 1}>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^{d + 1}\\)> dimensional parabolic SPDE and its <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^d>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^d\\)> dimensional invariant measure. As such, dimensional reduction of elliptic SPDEs can be considered a sort of elliptic stochastic quantisation procedure in the sense of Nelson (1966) and Parisi and Wu (1981). Our proof uses in a fundamental way the representation of the law of the SPDE as a supersymmetric quantum field theory. Dimensional reduction for the supersymmetric theory was already established by Klein et al. (1984). We fix a subtle gap in their proof and also complete the dimensional reduction picture by providing the link between the elliptic SPDE and the supersymmetric model. Even in our <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d = 0>><specific|html*|\\(d = 0\\)> context the arguments are non-trivial and a non-supersymmetric, elementary proof seems only to be available in the Gaussian case.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 11 December 2018; version 2 (arXiv v2), 5 August 2019<next-line>arXiv comments: 51 pages, an appendix added<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|0ecc3af100e45ed6cd807750b76ebfc0...>|ocfl/elliptic-stochastic-quantisation/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Paracontrolled approach to the three-dimensional stochastic nonlinear wave equation with quadratic nonlinearity|paper/stochastic-wave-3d/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli, Herbert Koch and Tadahiro Oh.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/stochastic-wave-3d/v3/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/stochastic-wave-3d/v3/content/source/1811.07808.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1811.07808|https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.07808>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        Using ideas from paracontrolled calculus, we prove local well-posedness of a renormalized version of the three-dimensional stochastic nonlinear wave equation with quadratic nonlinearity forced by an additive space-time white noise on a periodic domain. There are two new ingredients as compared to the parabolic setting. (i) In constructing stochastic objects, we have to carefully exploit dispersion at a multilinear level. (ii) We introduce novel random operators and leverage their regularity to overcome the lack of smoothing of usual paradifferential commutators.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        Using ideas from paracontrolled calculus, we prove local well-posedness of a renormalized version of the three-dimensional stochastic nonlinear wave equation with quadratic nonlinearity forced by an additive space-time white noise on a periodic domain. There are two new ingredients as compared to the parabolic setting. (i) In constructing stochastic objects, we have to carefully exploit dispersion at a multilinear level. (ii) We introduce novel random operators and leverage their regularity to overcome the lack of smoothing of usual paradifferential commutators.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 19 November 2018; version 3 (arXiv v3), 22 June 2021<next-line>arXiv comments: 52 pages. To appear in J. Eur. Math. Soc. Minor updates<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|113f3bc9888bccc6df817615a39cb31b...>|ocfl/stochastic-wave-3d/v4/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|The infinitesimal generator of the stochastic Burgers equation|paper/burgers-generator/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli and Nicolas Perkowski.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/burgers-generator/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/burgers-generator/v1/content/source/burgers-generator-3-max.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1810.12014|https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.12014>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We develop a martingale approach for a class of singular stochastic PDEs of Burgers type (including fractional and multi-component Burgers equations) by constructing a domain for their infinitesimal generators. It was known that the domain must have trivial intersection with the usual cylinder test functions, and to overcome this difficulty we import some ideas from paracontrolled distributions to an infinite dimensional setting in order to construct a domain of controlled functions. Using the new domain, we are able to prove existence and uniqueness for the Kolmogorov backward equation and the martingale problem. We also extend the uniqueness result for "energy solutions" of the stochastic Burgers equation of [GP18a] to a wider class of equations.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We develop a martingale approach for a class of singular stochastic PDEs of Burgers type (including fractional and multi-component Burgers equations) by constructing a domain for their infinitesimal generators. It was known that the domain must have trivial intersection with the usual cylinder test functions, and to overcome this difficulty we import some ideas from paracontrolled distributions to an infinite dimensional setting in order to construct a domain of controlled functions. Using the new domain, we are able to prove existence and uniqueness for the Kolmogorov backward equation and the martingale problem. We also extend the uniqueness result for "energy solutions" of the stochastic Burgers equation of [GP18a] to a wider class of equations.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 29 October 2018; version 1 (arXiv v1), 29 October 2018<next-line>arXiv comments: 47 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|57693837a24c52c6eca6596fe91f37f5...>|ocfl/burgers-generator/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|A PDE construction of the Euclidean <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^4_3>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^4_3\\)> quantum field theory|paper/phi43-pde-construction/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli and Martina Hofmanova.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/phi43-pde-construction/v4/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/phi43-pde-construction/v4/content/source/tightness-21.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1810.01700|https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.01700>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We present a new construction of the Euclidean <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^4>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^4\\)> quantum field theory on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^3>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^3\\)> based on PDE arguments. More precisely, we consider an approximation of the stochastic quantization equation on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^3>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^3\\)> defined on a periodic lattice of mesh size <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\varepsilon>><specific|html*|\\(\\varepsilon\\)> and side length <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|M>><specific|html*|\\(M\\)>. We introduce a new renormalized energy method in weighted spaces and prove tightness of the corresponding Gibbs measures as <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\varepsilon \\rightarrow 0>><specific|html*|\\(\\varepsilon \\rightarrow 0\\)>, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|M \\rightarrow \\infty>><specific|html*|\\(M \\rightarrow \\infty\\)>. Every limit point is non-Gaussian and satisfies reflection positivity, translation invariance and stretched exponential integrability. These properties allow to verify the Osterwalder\<#2013\>Schrader axioms for a Euclidean QFT apart from rotation invariance and clustering. Our argument applies to arbitrary positive coupling constant, to multicomponent models with <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|O(N)>><specific|html*|\\(O(N)\\)> symmetry and to some long-range variants. Moreover, we establish an integration by parts formula leading to the hierarchy of Dyson\<#2013\>Schwinger equations for the Euclidean correlation functions. To this end, we identify the renormalized cubic term as a distribution on the space of Euclidean fields.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We present a new construction of the Euclidean <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^4>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^4\\)> quantum field theory on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^3>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^3\\)> based on PDE arguments. More precisely, we consider an approximation of the stochastic quantization equation on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\mathbb{R}^3>><specific|html*|\\(\\mathbb{R}^3\\)> defined on a periodic lattice of mesh size <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\varepsilon>><specific|html*|\\(\\varepsilon\\)> and side length <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|M>><specific|html*|\\(M\\)>. We introduce a new renormalized energy method in weighted spaces and prove tightness of the corresponding Gibbs measures as <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\varepsilon \\rightarrow 0>><specific|html*|\\(\\varepsilon \\rightarrow 0\\)>, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|M \\rightarrow \\infty>><specific|html*|\\(M \\rightarrow \\infty\\)>. Every limit point is non-Gaussian and satisfies reflection positivity, translation invariance and stretched exponential integrability. These properties allow to verify the Osterwalder\<#2013\>Schrader axioms for a Euclidean QFT apart from rotation invariance and clustering. Our argument applies to arbitrary positive coupling constant, to multicomponent models with <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|O(N)>><specific|html*|\\(O(N)\\)> symmetry and to some long-range variants. Moreover, we establish an integration by parts formula leading to the hierarchy of Dyson\<#2013\>Schwinger equations for the Euclidean correlation functions. To this end, we identify the renormalized cubic term as a distribution on the space of Euclidean fields.
    </html-div-class>>>

