    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|A variational method for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^4_3>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^4_3\\)>|paper/phi43-variational/>>

    By N. Barashkov and M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|HTML|site/phi43-variational/phi43-variational.html> \<cdot\> <hlink|PDF|ocfl/phi43-variational/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <specific|texmacs|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/phi43-variational/v2/content/texmacs/variational-qft-16.tm>><specific|html*|<hlink|TeXmacs source|ocfl/phi43-variational/v2/content/texmacs/variational-qft-16.texmacs>> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1805.10814|https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.10814>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We introduce an explicit description of the <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^4_3>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^4_3\\)> measure on a bounded domain. Our starting point is the interpretation of its Laplace transform as the value function of a stochastic optimal control problem along the flow of a scale regularization parameter. Once small scale singularities have been renormalized by the standard counterterms, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\Gamma\\)>-convergence allows to extend the variational characterization to the unregularized model.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We introduce an explicit description of the <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Phi^4_3>><specific|html*|\\(\\Phi^4_3\\)> measure on a bounded domain. Our starting point is the interpretation of its Laplace transform as the value function of a stochastic optimal control problem along the flow of a scale regularization parameter. Once small scale singularities have been renormalized by the standard counterterms, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\Gamma\\)>-convergence allows to extend the variational characterization to the unregularized model.
    </html-div-class>>>

