  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Energy solutions of KPZ are unique|paper/kpz-energy-uniqueness/>>

    By M. Gubinelli and N. Perkowski.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/kpz-energy-uniqueness/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/kpz-energy-uniqueness/v2/content/source/1508.07764.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1508.07764|https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.07764>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        The Kardar-Parisi-Zhang (KPZ) equation is conjectured to universally describe the fluctuations of weakly asymmetric interface growth. Here we provide the first intrinsic well-posedness result for the KPZ equation on the real line by showing that its energy solutions (as introduced by Gonçalves and Jara and later refined by Gubinelli and Jara) are unique. Together with various convergence results already present in the literature, this establishes the weak KPZ universality conjecture for a wide class of models. A remarkable consequence is that the energy solution to the KPZ equation is not equal to the Cole-Hopf solution, but it involves an additional drift <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|t/12>><specific|html*|\\(t/12\\)>.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        The Kardar-Parisi-Zhang (KPZ) equation is conjectured to universally describe the fluctuations of weakly asymmetric interface growth. Here we provide the first intrinsic well-posedness result for the KPZ equation on the real line by showing that its energy solutions (as introduced by Gonçalves and Jara and later refined by Gubinelli and Jara) are unique. Together with various convergence results already present in the literature, this establishes the weak KPZ universality conjecture for a wide class of models. A remarkable consequence is that the energy solution to the KPZ equation is not equal to the Cole-Hopf solution, but it involves an additional drift <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|t/12>><specific|html*|\\(t/12\\)>.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 31 August 2015; version 2 (arXiv v2), 8 August 2016<next-line>arXiv comments: 38 pages, added the proof in the case of the full line<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|3e24323a634dc1066777bd63275003fc...>|ocfl/kpz-energy-uniqueness/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|KPZ reloaded|paper/kpz-reloaded/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli and Nicolas Perkowski.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/kpz-reloaded/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/kpz-reloaded/v1/content/source/kpz-reloaded-14-nocomments.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1508.03877|https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.03877>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We analyze the one-dimensional periodic Kardar-Parisi-Zhang equation in the language of paracontrolled distributions, giving an alternative viewpoint on the seminal results of Hairer. Apart from deriving a basic existence and uniqueness result for paracontrolled solutions to the KPZ equation we perform a thorough study of some related problems. We rigorously prove the links between KPZ equation, stochastic Burgers equation, and (linear) stochastic heat equation and also the existence of solutions starting from quite irregular initial conditions. We also build a partial link between energy solutions as introduced by Goncalves and Jara and paracontrolled solutions. Interpreting the KPZ equation as the value function of an optimal control problem, we give a pathwise proof for the global existence of solutions and thus for the strict positivity of solutions to the stochastic heat equation. Moreover, we study Sasamoto-Spohn type discretizations of the stochastic Burgers equation and show that their limit solves the continuous Burgers equation possibly with an additional linear transport term. As an application, we give a proof of the invariance of the white noise for the stochastic Burgers equation which does not rely on the Cole-Hopf transform.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We analyze the one-dimensional periodic Kardar-Parisi-Zhang equation in the language of paracontrolled distributions, giving an alternative viewpoint on the seminal results of Hairer. Apart from deriving a basic existence and uniqueness result for paracontrolled solutions to the KPZ equation we perform a thorough study of some related problems. We rigorously prove the links between KPZ equation, stochastic Burgers equation, and (linear) stochastic heat equation and also the existence of solutions starting from quite irregular initial conditions. We also build a partial link between energy solutions as introduced by Goncalves and Jara and paracontrolled solutions. Interpreting the KPZ equation as the value function of an optimal control problem, we give a pathwise proof for the global existence of solutions and thus for the strict positivity of solutions to the stochastic heat equation. Moreover, we study Sasamoto-Spohn type discretizations of the stochastic Burgers equation and show that their limit solves the continuous Burgers equation possibly with an additional linear transport term. As an application, we give a proof of the invariance of the white noise for the stochastic Burgers equation which does not rely on the Cole-Hopf transform.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 16 August 2015; version 1 (arXiv v1), 16 August 2015<next-line>arXiv comments: 111 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|288a7198d543df4daee9a62d383cd4e4...>|ocfl/kpz-reloaded/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Lectures on singular stochastic PDEs|paper/singular-spde-lectures/>>

    By M. Gubinelli and N. Perkowski.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/singular-spde-lectures/v3/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/singular-spde-lectures/v3/content/source/lectures-ebp-ensaios-math-revision.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1502.00157|https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.00157>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        These are the notes for a course at the 18th Brazilian School of Probability held from August 3rd to 9th, 2014 in Mambucaba. The aim of the course is to introduce the basic problems of non\<#2013\>linear PDEs with stochastic and irregular terms. We explain how it is possible to handle them using two main techniques: the notion of energy solutions and that of paracontrolled distributions. In order to maintain a link with physical intuitions, we motivate such singular SPDEs via a homogenisation result for a diffusion in a random potential.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        These are the notes for a course at the 18th Brazilian School of Probability held from August 3rd to 9th, 2014 in Mambucaba. The aim of the course is to introduce the basic problems of non\<#2013\>linear PDEs with stochastic and irregular terms. We explain how it is possible to handle them using two main techniques: the notion of energy solutions and that of paracontrolled distributions. In order to maintain a link with physical intuitions, we motivate such singular SPDEs via a homogenisation result for a diffusion in a random potential.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 31 January 2015; version 3 (arXiv v2), 29 July 2017<next-line>arXiv comments: 73 pages, 4 figures<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|777be179553095dbbf02f1087376a025...>|ocfl/singular-spde-lectures/v4/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Unbounded rough drivers|paper/unbounded-rough-drivers/>>

    By I. Bailleul and M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/unbounded-rough-drivers/v4/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/unbounded-rough-drivers/v4/content/source/1501.02074.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1501.02074|https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.02074>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We propose a theory of linear differential equations driven by unbounded operator-valued rough signals. As an application we consider rough linear transport equations and more general linear hyperbolic symmetric systems of equations driven by time-dependent vector fields which are only distributions in the time direction.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We propose a theory of linear differential equations driven by unbounded operator-valued rough signals. As an application we consider rough linear transport equations and more general linear hyperbolic symmetric systems of equations driven by time-dependent vector fields which are only distributions in the time direction.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 9 January 2015; version 4 (arXiv v4), 23 June 2017<next-line>arXiv comments: 38 pages. some improvements and precisions<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|5088860f57ccf11c54517b828ccdefc0...>|ocfl/unbounded-rough-drivers/v4/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Nonlinear PDEs with modulated dispersion II: Korteweg-de Vries equation|paper/modulated-dispersion-2/>>

    By Khalil Chouk, Massimiliano Gubinelli, Guopeng Li, Jiawei Li and Tadahiro Oh.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-2/v5/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-2/v5/content/source/modKdV_MEMS_accepted_arXiv.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1406.7675|https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.7675>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        (Due to the limit on the number of characters for an abstract set by arXiv, the full abstract can not be displayed here. See the abstract in the paper.) We study dispersive equations with a time non-homogeneous modulation acting on the linear dispersion term. As primary models, we consider the Korteweg-de Vries equation (KdV) and related equations such as the Benjamin-Ono equation (BO) and the intermediate long wave equation (ILW), imposing certain irregularity conditions on the time non-homogeneous modulation. In this work, we establish phenomena called regularization by noise in three-folds: (i) When the modulation is sufficiently irregular, we show that the modulated KdV on both the circle and the real line is locally well-posed in the regime where the (unmodulated) KdV equation is known to be ill-posed. In particular, given any <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|s \\in \\mathbb R>><specific|html*|\\(s \\in \\mathbb R\\)>, we show that the modulated KdV on the circle with a sufficiently irregular modulation is locally well-posed in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H^s(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(H^s(\\mathbb T)\\)>. Moreover, by adapting the <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|I>><specific|html*|\\(I\\)>-method to the current modulated setting, we prove global well-posedness of the modulated KdV in negative Sobolev spaces. (ii) It is known that certain (semilinear) dispersive equations such as BO and ILW exhibit quasilinear nature. We show that sufficiently irregular modulations make the modulated versions of these equations semilinear by establishing their local well-posedness by a contraction argument, providing local Lipschitz continuity of the solution map. (iii) We also prove nonlinear smoothing for these modulated equations, where we show that a gain of regularity of the nonlinear part becomes (arbitrarily) larger for more irregular modulations. As applications of our approach, we also include further examples.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        (Due to the limit on the number of characters for an abstract set by arXiv, the full abstract can not be displayed here. See the abstract in the paper.) We study dispersive equations with a time non-homogeneous modulation acting on the linear dispersion term. As primary models, we consider the Korteweg-de Vries equation (KdV) and related equations such as the Benjamin-Ono equation (BO) and the intermediate long wave equation (ILW), imposing certain irregularity conditions on the time non-homogeneous modulation. In this work, we establish phenomena called regularization by noise in three-folds: (i) When the modulation is sufficiently irregular, we show that the modulated KdV on both the circle and the real line is locally well-posed in the regime where the (unmodulated) KdV equation is known to be ill-posed. In particular, given any <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|s \\in \\mathbb R>><specific|html*|\\(s \\in \\mathbb R\\)>, we show that the modulated KdV on the circle with a sufficiently irregular modulation is locally well-posed in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H^s(\\mathbb T)>><specific|html*|\\(H^s(\\mathbb T)\\)>. Moreover, by adapting the <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|I>><specific|html*|\\(I\\)>-method to the current modulated setting, we prove global well-posedness of the modulated KdV in negative Sobolev spaces. (ii) It is known that certain (semilinear) dispersive equations such as BO and ILW exhibit quasilinear nature. We show that sufficiently irregular modulations make the modulated versions of these equations semilinear by establishing their local well-posedness by a contraction argument, providing local Lipschitz continuity of the solution map. (iii) We also prove nonlinear smoothing for these modulated equations, where we show that a gain of regularity of the nonlinear part becomes (arbitrarily) larger for more irregular modulations. As applications of our approach, we also include further examples.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 30 June 2014; version 5 (arXiv v5), 5 November 2026<next-line>arXiv comments: 94 pages. Updated funding info. To appear in Mem. Eur. Math. Soc<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|e8c7d582953168fac86f3d96db2affe7...>|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-2/v5/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Rough sheets|paper/rough-sheets/>>

    By K. Chouk and M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/rough-sheets/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/rough-sheets/v1/content/source/1406.7748.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1406.7748|https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.7748>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        Rough sheets are two-parameter analogs of rough paths. In this work the theory of integration over functions of two parameters is extended to cover the case of irregular functions by developing an appropriate notion of rough sheet. The main application is to give a path by path construction of the stochastic integral in the plane and obtain a stratonovich change of variables formula.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        Rough sheets are two-parameter analogs of rough paths. In this work the theory of integration over functions of two parameters is extended to cover the case of irregular functions by developing an appropriate notion of rough sheet. The main application is to give a path by path construction of the stochastic integral in the plane and obtain a stratonovich change of variables formula.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 30 June 2014; version 1 (arXiv v1), 30 June 2014<next-line>arXiv comments: 53 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|ab56cbeb0a711ade744f3717c4737121...>|ocfl/rough-sheets/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Controlled viscosity solutions of fully nonlinear rough PDEs|paper/controlled-viscosity-solutions/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli, Samy Tindel and Iván Torrecilla.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/controlled-viscosity-solutions/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/controlled-viscosity-solutions/v1/content/source/fully-ctrld9.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1403.2832|https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.2832>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We propose a definition of viscosity solutions to fully nonlinear PDEs driven by a rough path via appropriate notions of test functions and rough jets. These objects will be defined as controlled processes with respect to the driving rough path. We show that this notion is compatible with the seminal results of Lions and Souganidis and with the recent results of Friz and coauthors on fully non-linear SPDEs with rough drivers.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We propose a definition of viscosity solutions to fully nonlinear PDEs driven by a rough path via appropriate notions of test functions and rough jets. These objects will be defined as controlled processes with respect to the driving rough path. We show that this notion is compatible with the seminal results of Lions and Souganidis and with the recent results of Friz and coauthors on fully non-linear SPDEs with rough drivers.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 12 March 2014; version 1 (arXiv v1), 12 March 2014<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|d0de11eff2b330d85e3ba9785beded73...>|ocfl/controlled-viscosity-solutions/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Stochastic ODEs and stochastic linear PDEs with critical drift: regularity, duality and uniqueness|paper/critical-drift-regularity/>>

    By Lisa Beck, Franco Flandoli, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Mario Maurelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/critical-drift-regularity/v3/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/critical-drift-regularity/v3/content/source/BFGM_2019_10_29.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1401.1530|https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.1530>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        In this paper linear stochastic transport and continuity equations with drift in critical <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^{p}>><specific|html*|\\(L^{p}\\)> spaces are considered. In this situation noise prevents shocks for the transport equation and singularities in the density for the continuity equation, starting from smooth initial conditions. Specifically, we first prove a result of Sobolev regularity of solutions, which is false for the corresponding deterministic equation. The technique needed to reach the critical case is new and based on parabolic equations satisfied by moments of first derivatives of the solution, opposite to previous works based on stochastic flows. The approach extends to higher order derivatives under more regularity of the drift term. By a duality approach, these regularity results are then applied to prove uniqueness of weak solutions to linear stochastic continuity and transport equations and certain well-posedness results for the associated stochastic differential equation (sDE) (roughly speaking, existence and uniqueness of flows and their <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|C^\\alpha>><specific|html*|\\(C^\\alpha\\)> regularity, strong uniqueness for the sDE when the initial datum has diffuse law). Finally, we show two types of examples: on the one hand, we present well-posed sDEs, when the corresponding ODEs are ill-posed, and on the other hand, we give a counterexample in the supercritical case.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        In this paper linear stochastic transport and continuity equations with drift in critical <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^{p}>><specific|html*|\\(L^{p}\\)> spaces are considered. In this situation noise prevents shocks for the transport equation and singularities in the density for the continuity equation, starting from smooth initial conditions. Specifically, we first prove a result of Sobolev regularity of solutions, which is false for the corresponding deterministic equation. The technique needed to reach the critical case is new and based on parabolic equations satisfied by moments of first derivatives of the solution, opposite to previous works based on stochastic flows. The approach extends to higher order derivatives under more regularity of the drift term. By a duality approach, these regularity results are then applied to prove uniqueness of weak solutions to linear stochastic continuity and transport equations and certain well-posedness results for the associated stochastic differential equation (sDE) (roughly speaking, existence and uniqueness of flows and their <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|C^\\alpha>><specific|html*|\\(C^\\alpha\\)> regularity, strong uniqueness for the sDE when the initial datum has diffuse law). Finally, we show two types of examples: on the one hand, we present well-posed sDEs, when the corresponding ODEs are ill-posed, and on the other hand, we give a counterexample in the supercritical case.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 7 January 2014; version 3 (arXiv v3), 29 October 2019<next-line>arXiv comments: 64 pages, 1 figure, comments are welcome<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|79969383012aa42fb177e89f6f5c703f...>|ocfl/critical-drift-regularity/v3/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Ultraviolet Renormalization of the Nelson Hamiltonian through Functional Integration|paper/nelson-uv-renormalisation/>>

    By M. Gubinelli, F. Hiroshima and J. Lorinczi.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/nelson-uv-renormalisation/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/nelson-uv-renormalisation/v2/content/source/1304.6662.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1304.6662|https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.6662>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        Starting from the N-particle Nelson Hamiltonian defined by imposing an ultraviolet cutoff, we perform ultraviolet renormalization by showing that in the zero cutoff limit a self-adjoint operator exists after a logarithmically divergent term is subtracted from the original Hamiltonian. We obtain this term as the diagonal part of a pair interaction appearing in the density of a Gibbs measure derived from the Feynman-Kac representation of the Hamiltonian. Also, we show existence of a weak coupling limit of the renormalized Hamiltonian and derive an effective Yukawa interaction potential between the particles.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        Starting from the N-particle Nelson Hamiltonian defined by imposing an ultraviolet cutoff, we perform ultraviolet renormalization by showing that in the zero cutoff limit a self-adjoint operator exists after a logarithmically divergent term is subtracted from the original Hamiltonian. We obtain this term as the diagonal part of a pair interaction appearing in the density of a Gibbs measure derived from the Feynman-Kac representation of the Hamiltonian. Also, we show existence of a weak coupling limit of the renormalized Hamiltonian and derive an effective Yukawa interaction potential between the particles.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 24 April 2013; version 2 (arXiv v2), 4 October 2013<next-line>arXiv comments: 28 pages, revision of section 2 and typos corrected<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|31f8d03ca447774d459f29444998d73f...>|ocfl/nelson-uv-renormalisation/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Nonlinear PDEs with modulated dispersion I: Nonlinear Schrödinger equations|paper/modulated-dispersion-1/>>

    By K. Chouk and M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-1/v3/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-1/v3/content/source/1303.0822.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1303.0822|https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.0822>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We start a study of various nonlinear PDEs under the effect of a modulation in time of the dispersive term. In particular in this paper we consider the modulated non-linear Schrödinger equation (NLS) in dimension 1 and 2 and the derivative NLS in dimension 1. We introduce a deterministic notion of "irregularity" for the modulation and obtain local and global results similar to those valid without modulation. In some situations, we show how the irregularity of the modulation improves the well\<#2013\>posedness theory of the equations. We develop two different approaches to the analysis of the effects of the modulation. A first approach is based on novel estimates for the regularising effect of the modulated dispersion on the non-linear term using the theory of controlled paths. A second approach is an extension of a Strichartz estimated first obtained by Debussche and Tsutsumi in the case of the Brownian modulation for the quintic NLS.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We start a study of various nonlinear PDEs under the effect of a modulation in time of the dispersive term. In particular in this paper we consider the modulated non-linear Schrödinger equation (NLS) in dimension 1 and 2 and the derivative NLS in dimension 1. We introduce a deterministic notion of "irregularity" for the modulation and obtain local and global results similar to those valid without modulation. In some situations, we show how the irregularity of the modulation improves the well\<#2013\>posedness theory of the equations. We develop two different approaches to the analysis of the effects of the modulation. A first approach is based on novel estimates for the regularising effect of the modulated dispersion on the non-linear term using the theory of controlled paths. A second approach is an extension of a Strichartz estimated first obtained by Debussche and Tsutsumi in the case of the Brownian modulation for the quintic NLS.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 4 March 2013; version 3 (arXiv v3), 29 January 2015<next-line>arXiv comments: 27 pages. Extensive reorganisation of the material and typos corrected<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|8416442081c11b0ffa6fb6b1ff443f43...>|ocfl/modulated-dispersion-1/v4/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Remarks on the stochastic transport equation with Hölder drift|paper/stochastic-transport-holder/>>

    By Franco Flandoli, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Enrico Priola.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/stochastic-transport-holder/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/stochastic-transport-holder/v1/content/source/1301.4012.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1301.4012|https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.4012>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We consider a stochastic linear transport equation with a globally Hölder continuous and bounded vector field. Opposite to what happens in the deterministic case where shocks may appear, we show that the unique solution starting with a C^1-initial condition remains of class <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|C^{1}>><specific|html*|\\(C^{1}\\)> in space. We also improve some results of Flandoli-Gubinelli-Priola (2009) about well-posedness. Moreover, we prove a stability property for the solution with respect to the initial datum.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We consider a stochastic linear transport equation with a globally Hölder continuous and bounded vector field. Opposite to what happens in the deterministic case where shocks may appear, we show that the unique solution starting with a C^1-initial condition remains of class <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|C^{1}>><specific|html*|\\(C^{1}\\)> in space. We also improve some results of Flandoli-Gubinelli-Priola (2009) about well-posedness. Moreover, we prove a stability property for the solution with respect to the initial datum.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 17 January 2013; version 1 (arXiv v1), 17 January 2013<next-line>arXiv comments: arXiv admin note: text overlap with arXiv:0809.1310<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|e10300b0e25c4fd2baf3f5c12e1193dc...>|ocfl/stochastic-transport-holder/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Paracontrolled distributions and singular PDEs|paper/paracontrolled-distributions/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli, Peter Imkeller and Nicolas Perkowski.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/paracontrolled-distributions/v5/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/paracontrolled-distributions/v5/content/source/1210.2684.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1210.2684|https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.2684>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We introduce an approach to study certain singular PDEs which is based on techniques from paradifferential calculus and on ideas from the theory of controlled rough paths. We illustrate its applicability on some model problems like differential equations driven by fractional Brownian motion, a fractional Burgers type SPDE driven by space-time white noise, and a non-linear version of the parabolic Anderson model with a white noise potential.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We introduce an approach to study certain singular PDEs which is based on techniques from paradifferential calculus and on ideas from the theory of controlled rough paths. We illustrate its applicability on some model problems like differential equations driven by fractional Brownian motion, a fractional Burgers type SPDE driven by space-time white noise, and a non-linear version of the parabolic Anderson model with a white noise potential.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 9 October 2012; version 5 (arXiv v4), 15 August 2017<next-line>arXiv comments: published version<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|4ec8cfcb5fec48405d25a78ff1d70ba7...>|ocfl/paracontrolled-distributions/v5/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Regularization by noise and stochastic Burgers equations|paper/burgers-regularisation-by-noise/>>

    By M. Gubinelli and M. Jara.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/burgers-regularisation-by-noise/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/burgers-regularisation-by-noise/v2/content/source/1208.6551.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1208.6551|https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.6551>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We study a generalized 1d periodic SPDE of Burgers type: <specific|texmacs|<verbatim| \\partial_t u =- A^\\theta u + \\partial_x u^2 + A^{\\theta/2} \\xi >><specific|html*|\\( \\partial_t u =- A^\\theta u + \\partial_x u^2 + A^{\\theta/2} \\xi \\)> where <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\theta <gtr> 1/2>><specific|html*|\\(\\theta <gtr> 1/2\\)>, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|-A>><specific|html*|\\(-A\\)> is the 1d Laplacian, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\xi>><specific|html*|\\(\\xi\\)> is a space-time white noise and the initial condition <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|u_0>><specific|html*|\\(u_0\\)> is taken to be (space) white noise. We introduce a notion of weak solution for this equation in the stationary setting. For these solutions we point out how the noise provide a regularizing effect allowing to prove existence and suitable estimates when <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\theta<gtr>1/2>><specific|html*|\\(\\theta<gtr>1/2\\)>. When <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\theta<gtr>5/4>><specific|html*|\\(\\theta<gtr>5/4\\)> we obtain pathwise uniqueness. We discuss the use of the same method to study different approximations of the same equation and for a model of stationary 2d stochastic Navier-Stokes evolution.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We study a generalized 1d periodic SPDE of Burgers type: <specific|texmacs|<verbatim| \\partial_t u =- A^\\theta u + \\partial_x u^2 + A^{\\theta/2} \\xi >><specific|html*|\\( \\partial_t u =- A^\\theta u + \\partial_x u^2 + A^{\\theta/2} \\xi \\)> where <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\theta <gtr> 1/2>><specific|html*|\\(\\theta <gtr> 1/2\\)>, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|-A>><specific|html*|\\(-A\\)> is the 1d Laplacian, <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\xi>><specific|html*|\\(\\xi\\)> is a space-time white noise and the initial condition <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|u_0>><specific|html*|\\(u_0\\)> is taken to be (space) white noise. We introduce a notion of weak solution for this equation in the stationary setting. For these solutions we point out how the noise provide a regularizing effect allowing to prove existence and suitable estimates when <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\theta<gtr>1/2>><specific|html*|\\(\\theta<gtr>1/2\\)>. When <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\theta<gtr>5/4>><specific|html*|\\(\\theta<gtr>5/4\\)> we obtain pathwise uniqueness. We discuss the use of the same method to study different approximations of the same equation and for a model of stationary 2d stochastic Navier-Stokes evolution.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 31 August 2012; version 2 (arXiv v2), 9 April 2013<next-line>arXiv comments: clarifications and small corrections<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|734ea2ba5a2b3bc54167eb603c811545...>|ocfl/burgers-regularisation-by-noise/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Averaging along irregular curves and regularisation of ODEs|paper/averaging-irregular-curves/>>

    By R. Catellier and M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/averaging-irregular-curves/v4/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/averaging-irregular-curves/v4/content/source/1205.1735.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1205.1735|https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.1735>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We consider the ordinary differential equation (ODE) <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|dx_{t} =b(t,x_{t} ) dt+ dw_{t}>><specific|html*|\\(dx_{t} =b(t,x_{t} ) dt+ dw_{t}\\)> where <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|w>><specific|html*|\\(w\\)> is a continuous driving function and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|b>><specific|html*|\\(b\\)> is a time-dependent vector field which possibly is only a distribution in the space variable. We quantify the regularising properties of an arbitrary continuous path <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|w>><specific|html*|\\(w\\)> on the existence and uniqueness of solutions to this equation. In this context we introduce the notion of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregularity and show that it plays a key role in some instances of the regularisation by noise phenomenon. In the particular case of a function <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|w>><specific|html*|\\(w\\)> sampled according to the law of the fractional Brownian motion of Hurst index <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H \\in (0,1)>><specific|html*|\\(H \\in (0,1)\\)>, we prove that almost surely the ODE admits a solution for all <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|b>><specific|html*|\\(b\\)> in the Besov\<#2013\>Hölder space <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|B^{\\alpha+1}_{\\infty , \\infty}>><specific|html*|\\(B^{\\alpha+1}_{\\infty , \\infty}\\)> with <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha <gtr>-1/2H>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha <gtr>-1/2H\\)>. If <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha <gtr>1-1/2H>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha <gtr>1-1/2H\\)> then the solution is unique among a natural set of continuous solutions. If <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H<gtr>1/3>><specific|html*|\\(H<gtr>1/3\\)> and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha <gtr>3/2-1/2H>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha <gtr>3/2-1/2H\\)> or if <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha <gtr>2-1/2H>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha <gtr>2-1/2H\\)> then the equation admits a unique Lipschitz flow. Note that when <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha <less>0>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha <less>0\\)> the vector field <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|b>><specific|html*|\\(b\\)> is only a distribution, nonetheless there exists a natural notion of solution for which the above results apply.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We consider the ordinary differential equation (ODE) <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|dx_{t} =b(t,x_{t} ) dt+ dw_{t}>><specific|html*|\\(dx_{t} =b(t,x_{t} ) dt+ dw_{t}\\)> where <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|w>><specific|html*|\\(w\\)> is a continuous driving function and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|b>><specific|html*|\\(b\\)> is a time-dependent vector field which possibly is only a distribution in the space variable. We quantify the regularising properties of an arbitrary continuous path <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|w>><specific|html*|\\(w\\)> on the existence and uniqueness of solutions to this equation. In this context we introduce the notion of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\rho>><specific|html*|\\(\\rho\\)>-irregularity and show that it plays a key role in some instances of the regularisation by noise phenomenon. In the particular case of a function <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|w>><specific|html*|\\(w\\)> sampled according to the law of the fractional Brownian motion of Hurst index <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H \\in (0,1)>><specific|html*|\\(H \\in (0,1)\\)>, we prove that almost surely the ODE admits a solution for all <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|b>><specific|html*|\\(b\\)> in the Besov\<#2013\>Hölder space <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|B^{\\alpha+1}_{\\infty , \\infty}>><specific|html*|\\(B^{\\alpha+1}_{\\infty , \\infty}\\)> with <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha <gtr>-1/2H>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha <gtr>-1/2H\\)>. If <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha <gtr>1-1/2H>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha <gtr>1-1/2H\\)> then the solution is unique among a natural set of continuous solutions. If <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|H<gtr>1/3>><specific|html*|\\(H<gtr>1/3\\)> and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha <gtr>3/2-1/2H>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha <gtr>3/2-1/2H\\)> or if <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha <gtr>2-1/2H>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha <gtr>2-1/2H\\)> then the equation admits a unique Lipschitz flow. Note that when <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\alpha <less>0>><specific|html*|\\(\\alpha <less>0\\)> the vector field <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|b>><specific|html*|\\(b\\)> is only a distribution, nonetheless there exists a natural notion of solution for which the above results apply.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 8 May 2012; version 4 (arXiv v4), 4 February 2016<next-line>arXiv comments: 49 pages, small typos and minor corrections<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|a6f0c38660729431d4b5ca82855679a7...>|ocfl/averaging-irregular-curves/v5/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Global evolution of random vortex filament equation|paper/random-vortex-filament-evolution/>>

    By Z. Brze\<#17A\>niak, M. Gubinelli and M. Neklyudov.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/random-vortex-filament-evolution/v5/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/random-vortex-filament-evolution/v5/content/source/1008.1086.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1008.1086|https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.1086>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We prove the existence of a global solution for the filament equation with inital condition given by a geometric rough path in the sense of Lyons (1998).Our work gives a positive answer to a question left open in recent publications: Berselli and Gubinelli (2007) showed the existence of global solution for a smooth initial condition while Bessaih, Gubinelli, Russo (2005) proved the existence of a local solution for a general initial condition given by a rough path.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We prove the existence of a global solution for the filament equation with inital condition given by a geometric rough path in the sense of Lyons (1998).Our work gives a positive answer to a question left open in recent publications: Berselli and Gubinelli (2007) showed the existence of global solution for a smooth initial condition while Bessaih, Gubinelli, Russo (2005) proved the existence of a local solution for a general initial condition given by a rough path.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 5 August 2010; version 5 (arXiv v5), 4 July 2013<next-line>arXiv comments: 19 pages; minor revisions<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|d42505b3d56028979c77c09770df1002...>|ocfl/random-vortex-filament-evolution/v5/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Full well-posedness of point vortex dynamics corresponding to stochastic 2D Euler equations|paper/point-vortices-noise/>>

    By F. Flandoli, M. Gubinelli and E. Priola.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/point-vortices-noise/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/point-vortices-noise/v1/content/source/1004.1407.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:1004.1407|https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.1407>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        The motion of a finite number of point vortices on a two-dimensional periodic domain is considered. In the deterministic case it is known to be well posed only for almost every initial configuration. Coalescence of vortices may occur for certain initial conditions. We prove that when a generic stochastic perturbation compatible with the Eulerian description is introduced, the point vortex motion becomes well posed for every initial configuration, in particular coalescence disappears.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        The motion of a finite number of point vortices on a two-dimensional periodic domain is considered. In the deterministic case it is known to be well posed only for almost every initial configuration. Coalescence of vortices may occur for certain initial conditions. We prove that when a generic stochastic perturbation compatible with the Eulerian description is introduced, the point vortex motion becomes well posed for every initial configuration, in particular coalescence disappears.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 8 April 2010; version 1 (arXiv v1), 8 April 2010<next-line>arXiv comments: 23 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|32fe07bec20476b3c1f525335fb1b7e9...>|ocfl/point-vortices-noise/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Non-linear Rough Heat Equations|paper/rough-heat-equations/>>

    By A. Deya, M. Gubinelli and S. Tindel.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/rough-heat-equations/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/rough-heat-equations/v1/content/source/0911.0618.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:0911.0618|https://arxiv.org/abs/0911.0618>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        This article is devoted to define and solve an evolution equation of the form <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|dy_t=\\Delta y_t dt+ dX_t(y_t)>><specific|html*|\\(dy_t=\\Delta y_t dt+ dX_t(y_t)\\)>, where <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Delta>><specific|html*|\\(\\Delta\\)> stands for the Laplace operator on a space of the form <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)>><specific|html*|\\(L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)\\)>, and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|X>><specific|html*|\\(X\\)> is a finite dimensional noisy nonlinearity whose typical form is given by <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|X_t(\\varphi)=\\sum_{i=1}^N x^{i}_t f_i(\\varphi)>><specific|html*|\\(X_t(\\varphi)=\\sum_{i=1}^N x^{i}_t f_i(\\varphi)\\)>, where each <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|x=(x^{(1)},...,x^{(N)})>><specific|html*|\\(x=(x^{(1)},...,x^{(N)})\\)> is a <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)>-Hölder function generating a rough path and each <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|f_i>><specific|html*|\\(f_i\\)> is a smooth enough function defined on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)>><specific|html*|\\(L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)\\)>. The generalization of the usual rough path theory allowing to cope with such kind of systems is carefully constructed.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        This article is devoted to define and solve an evolution equation of the form <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|dy_t=\\Delta y_t dt+ dX_t(y_t)>><specific|html*|\\(dy_t=\\Delta y_t dt+ dX_t(y_t)\\)>, where <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\Delta>><specific|html*|\\(\\Delta\\)> stands for the Laplace operator on a space of the form <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)>><specific|html*|\\(L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)\\)>, and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|X>><specific|html*|\\(X\\)> is a finite dimensional noisy nonlinearity whose typical form is given by <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|X_t(\\varphi)=\\sum_{i=1}^N x^{i}_t f_i(\\varphi)>><specific|html*|\\(X_t(\\varphi)=\\sum_{i=1}^N x^{i}_t f_i(\\varphi)\\)>, where each <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|x=(x^{(1)},...,x^{(N)})>><specific|html*|\\(x=(x^{(1)},...,x^{(N)})\\)> is a <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)>-Hölder function generating a rough path and each <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|f_i>><specific|html*|\\(f_i\\)> is a smooth enough function defined on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)>><specific|html*|\\(L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)\\)>. The generalization of the usual rough path theory allowing to cope with such kind of systems is carefully constructed.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 3 November 2009; version 1 (arXiv v1), 3 November 2009<next-line>arXiv comments: 36 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|fc9d238118e157c1eea6500cabe7b9b9...>|ocfl/rough-heat-equations/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Flow of diffeomorphisms for SDEs with unbounded Hölder continuous drift|paper/sde-flows-unbounded-drift/>>

    By F. Flandoli, M. Gubinelli and E. Priola.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/sde-flows-unbounded-drift/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/sde-flows-unbounded-drift/v1/content/source/0907.3668.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:0907.3668|https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.3668>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We consider a SDE with a smooth multiplicative non-degenerate noise and a possibly unbounded Holder continuous drift term. We prove existence of a global flow of diffeomorphisms by means of a special transformation of the drift of Ito-Tanaka type. The proof requires non-standard elliptic estimates in Holder spaces. As an application of the stochastic flow, we obtain a Bismut-Elworthy-Li type formula for the first derivatives of the associated diffusion semigroup.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We consider a SDE with a smooth multiplicative non-degenerate noise and a possibly unbounded Holder continuous drift term. We prove existence of a global flow of diffeomorphisms by means of a special transformation of the drift of Ito-Tanaka type. The proof requires non-standard elliptic estimates in Holder spaces. As an application of the stochastic flow, we obtain a Bismut-Elworthy-Li type formula for the first derivatives of the associated diffusion semigroup.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 21 July 2009; version 1 (arXiv v1), 21 July 2009<next-line>arXiv comments: 21 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|a4b15132f2caf93b78d8fe5d7223a229...>|ocfl/sde-flows-unbounded-drift/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Global existence for rough differential equations under linear growth conditions|paper/rde-global-existence/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli and Antoine Lejay.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/rde-global-existence/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/rde-global-existence/v1/content/source/0905.2399.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:0905.2399|https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.2399>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We prove existence of global solutions for differential equations driven by a geometric rough path under the condition that the vector fields have linear growth. We show by an explicit counter-example that the linear growth condition is not sufficient if the driving rough path is not geometric. This settle a long-standing open question in the theory of rough paths. So in the geometric setting we recover the usual sufficient condition for differential equation. The proof rely on a simple mapping of the differential equation from the Euclidean space to a manifold to obtain a rough differential equation with bounded coefficients.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We prove existence of global solutions for differential equations driven by a geometric rough path under the condition that the vector fields have linear growth. We show by an explicit counter-example that the linear growth condition is not sufficient if the driving rough path is not geometric. This settle a long-standing open question in the theory of rough paths. So in the geometric setting we recover the usual sufficient condition for differential equation. The proof rely on a simple mapping of the differential equation from the Euclidean space to a manifold to obtain a rough differential equation with bounded coefficients.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 14 May 2009; version 1 (arXiv v1), 14 May 2009<next-line>arXiv comments: 20 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|0e93197ef38f94c095616dea64da402b...>|ocfl/rde-global-existence/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Abstract integration, Combinatorics of Trees and Differential Equations|paper/abstract-integration-trees/>>

    By M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/abstract-integration-trees/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/abstract-integration-trees/v1/content/source/0809.1821.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:0809.1821|https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.1821>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        This is a review paper on recent work about the connections between rough path theory, the Connes-Kreimer Hopf algebra on rooted trees and the analysis of finite and infinite dimensional differential equation. We try to explain and motivate the theory of rough paths introduced by T. Lyons in the context of differential equations in presence of irregular noises. We show how it is used in an abstract algebraic approach to the definition of integrals over paths which involves a cochain complex of finite increments. In the context of such abstract integration theories we outline a connection with the combinatorics of rooted trees. As interesting examples where these ideas apply we present two infinite dimensional dynamical systems: the Navier-Stokes equation and the Korteweg-de-Vries equation.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        This is a review paper on recent work about the connections between rough path theory, the Connes-Kreimer Hopf algebra on rooted trees and the analysis of finite and infinite dimensional differential equation. We try to explain and motivate the theory of rough paths introduced by T. Lyons in the context of differential equations in presence of irregular noises. We show how it is used in an abstract algebraic approach to the definition of integrals over paths which involves a cochain complex of finite increments. In the context of such abstract integration theories we outline a connection with the combinatorics of rooted trees. As interesting examples where these ideas apply we present two infinite dimensional dynamical systems: the Navier-Stokes equation and the Korteweg-de-Vries equation.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 10 September 2008; version 1 (arXiv v1), 10 September 2008<next-line>arXiv comments: 17 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|bb03e0017ac659ce06e0184dd73e17da...>|ocfl/abstract-integration-trees/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Well-posedness of the transport equation by stochastic perturbation|paper/transport-noise-wellposedness/>>

    By Franco Flandoli, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Enrico Priola.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/transport-noise-wellposedness/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/transport-noise-wellposedness/v2/content/source/0809.1310.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:0809.1310|https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.1310>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We consider the linear transport equation with a globally Holder continuous and bounded vector field. While this deterministic PDE may not be well-posed, we prove that a multiplicative stochastic perturbation of Brownian type is enough to render the equation well-posed. This seems to be the first explicit example of partial differential equation that become well-posed under the influece of noise. The key tool is a differentiable stochastic flow constructed and analysed by means of a special transformation of the drift of Ito-Tanaka type.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We consider the linear transport equation with a globally Holder continuous and bounded vector field. While this deterministic PDE may not be well-posed, we prove that a multiplicative stochastic perturbation of Brownian type is enough to render the equation well-posed. This seems to be the first explicit example of partial differential equation that become well-posed under the influece of noise. The key tool is a differentiable stochastic flow constructed and analysed by means of a special transformation of the drift of Ito-Tanaka type.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 8 September 2008; version 2 (arXiv v2), 8 May 2009<next-line>arXiv comments: Addition of new parts<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|a84d9cd6bad95c8b52d8dda4b1ee4267...>|ocfl/transport-noise-wellposedness/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Large scale behavior of semiflexible heteropolymers|paper/semiflexible-heteropolymers/>>

    By Francesco Caravenna, Giambattista Giacomin and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/semiflexible-heteropolymers/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/semiflexible-heteropolymers/v2/content/source/CGG2.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:0807.4232|https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.4232>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We consider a general discrete model for heterogeneous semiflexible polymer chains. Both the thermal noise and the inhomogeneous character of the chain (the disorder) are modeled in terms of random rotations. We focus on the quenched regime, i.e., the analysis is performed for a given realization of the disorder. Semiflexible models differ substantially from random walks on short scales, but on large scales a Brownian behavior emerges. By exploiting techniques from tensor analysis and non-commutative Fourier analysis, we establish the Brownian character of the model on large scale and we obtain an expression for the diffusion constant. We moreover give conditions yielding quantitative mixing properties.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We consider a general discrete model for heterogeneous semiflexible polymer chains. Both the thermal noise and the inhomogeneous character of the chain (the disorder) are modeled in terms of random rotations. We focus on the quenched regime, i.e., the analysis is performed for a given realization of the disorder. Semiflexible models differ substantially from random walks on short scales, but on large scales a Brownian behavior emerges. By exploiting techniques from tensor analysis and non-commutative Fourier analysis, we establish the Brownian character of the model on large scale and we obtain an expression for the diffusion constant. We moreover give conditions yielding quantitative mixing properties.
    </html-div-class>>>

