  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Rough evolution equations|paper/rough-evolution-equations/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli and Samy Tindel.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/rough-evolution-equations/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:0803.0552|https://arxiv.org/abs/0803.0552>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We generalize Lyons' rough paths theory in order to give a pathwise meaning to some nonlinear infinite-dimensional evolution equation associated to an analytic semigroup and driven by an irregular noise. As an illustration, we discuss a class of linear and nonlinear 1d SPDEs driven by a space\<#2013\>time Gaussian noise with singular space covariance and Brownian time dependence.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We generalize Lyons' rough paths theory in order to give a pathwise meaning to some nonlinear infinite-dimensional evolution equation associated to an analytic semigroup and driven by an irregular noise. As an illustration, we discuss a class of linear and nonlinear 1d SPDEs driven by a space\<#2013\>time Gaussian noise with singular space covariance and Brownian time dependence.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 4 March 2008; version 2 (arXiv v2), 26 January 2010<next-line>arXiv comments: Published in at http://dx.doi.org/10.1214/08-AOP437 the Annals of Probability (http://www.imstat.org/aop/) by the Institute of Mathematical Statistics (http://www.imstat.org)<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|a023f46d57d7f54d8b733ca227354a48...>|ocfl/rough-evolution-equations/v3/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Gibbs measures on Brownian currents|paper/gibbs-brownian-currents/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli and Jozsef Lorinczi.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/gibbs-brownian-currents/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/gibbs-brownian-currents/v1/content/source/0704.3237.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:0704.3237|https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.3237>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        Motivated by applications to quantum field theory we consider Gibbs measures for which the reference measure is Wiener measure and the interaction is given by a double stochastic integral and a pinning external potential. In order properly to characterize these measures through DLR equations, we are led to lift Wiener measure and other objects to a space of configurations where the basic observables are not only the position of the particle at all times but also the work done by test vector fields. We prove existence and basic properties of such Gibbs measures in the small coupling regime by means of cluster expansion.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        Motivated by applications to quantum field theory we consider Gibbs measures for which the reference measure is Wiener measure and the interaction is given by a double stochastic integral and a pinning external potential. In order properly to characterize these measures through DLR equations, we are led to lift Wiener measure and other objects to a space of configurations where the basic observables are not only the position of the particle at all times but also the work done by test vector fields. We prove existence and basic properties of such Gibbs measures in the small coupling regime by means of cluster expansion.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 24 April 2007; version 1 (arXiv v1), 24 April 2007<next-line>arXiv comments: 51 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|85182a07beffd8efe5909efe31ab2368...>|ocfl/gibbs-brownian-currents/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Ramification of rough paths|paper/ramification-rough-paths/>>

    By M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/ramification-rough-paths/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/ramification-rough-paths/v1/content/source/math-0610300.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math/0610300|https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0610300>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        The stack of iterated integrals of a path is embedded in a larger algebraic structure where iterated integrals are indexed by decorated rooted trees and where an extended Chen's multiplicative property involves the Dürr-Connes-Kreimer coproduct on rooted trees. This turns out to be the natural setting for a non-geometric theory of rough paths.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        The stack of iterated integrals of a path is embedded in a larger algebraic structure where iterated integrals are indexed by decorated rooted trees and where an extended Chen's multiplicative property involves the Dürr-Connes-Kreimer coproduct on rooted trees. This turns out to be the natural setting for a non-geometric theory of rough paths.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 9 October 2006; version 1 (arXiv v1), 9 October 2006<next-line>arXiv comments: 28 pages<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|72154a0a72630e5a0b289f306c70cd9b...>|ocfl/ramification-rough-paths/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Rough solutions for the periodic Korteweg-de Vries equation|paper/rough-periodic-kdv/>>

    By M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/rough-periodic-kdv/v3/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/rough-periodic-kdv/v3/content/source/math-0610006.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math/0610006|https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0610006>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We show how to apply ideas from the theory of rough paths to the analysis of low-regularity solutions to non-linear dispersive equations. Our basic example will be the one dimensional Korteweg\<#2013\>de Vries (KdV) equation on a periodic domain and with initial condition in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|{\\mathcal{F}} L^{\\alpha,p}>><specific|html*|\\({\\mathcal{F}} L^{\\alpha,p}\\)> spaces. We discuss convergence of Galerkin approximations, a modified Euler scheme and the presence of a random force of white-noise type in time.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We show how to apply ideas from the theory of rough paths to the analysis of low-regularity solutions to non-linear dispersive equations. Our basic example will be the one dimensional Korteweg\<#2013\>de Vries (KdV) equation on a periodic domain and with initial condition in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|{\\mathcal{F}} L^{\\alpha,p}>><specific|html*|\\({\\mathcal{F}} L^{\\alpha,p}\\)> spaces. We discuss convergence of Galerkin approximations, a modified Euler scheme and the presence of a random force of white-noise type in time.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 30 September 2006; version 3 (arXiv v3), 20 July 2009<next-line>arXiv comments: 24 pages, no figures, major changes and discussion of $FL^{\<#3B1\>,p}$ spaces<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|3376b14a0153428b041622e9725e71ee...>|ocfl/rough-periodic-kdv/v4/inventory.json>>
