  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|On the global evolution of vortex filaments, blobs, and small loops in 3D ideal flows|paper/vortex-filaments-blobs-loops/>>

    By L. C. Berselli and M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/vortex-filaments-blobs-loops/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/vortex-filaments-blobs-loops/v1/content/source/math-ph-0510091.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math-ph/0510091|https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0510091>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We consider a wide class of approximate models of evolution of singular distributions of vorticity in three dimensional incompressible fluids and we show that they have global smooth solutions. The proof exploits the existence of suitable Hamiltonian functions. The approximate models we analyze (essentially discrete and continuous vortex filaments and vortex loops) are related to some problem of classical physics concerning turbulence and also to the numerical approximation of flows with very high Reynolds number. Finally, we interpret our results as a basis to theoretical validation of numerical methods used in state-of-the-art computations of turbulent flows.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We consider a wide class of approximate models of evolution of singular distributions of vorticity in three dimensional incompressible fluids and we show that they have global smooth solutions. The proof exploits the existence of suitable Hamiltonian functions. The approximate models we analyze (essentially discrete and continuous vortex filaments and vortex loops) are related to some problem of classical physics concerning turbulence and also to the numerical approximation of flows with very high Reynolds number. Finally, we interpret our results as a basis to theoretical validation of numerical methods used in state-of-the-art computations of turbulent flows.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 28 October 2005; version 1 (arXiv v1), 28 October 2005<next-line>arXiv comments: 22 pages, no figures<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|b7b0d4b21e4946d7a96be6ee1dba6e3d...>|ocfl/vortex-filaments-blobs-loops/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|A numerical approach to copolymers at selective interfaces|paper/copolymers-selective-interfaces/>>

    By F. Caravenna, G. Giacomin and M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/copolymers-selective-interfaces/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/copolymers-selective-interfaces/v1/content/source/CGG_JSP.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math-ph/0509065|https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0509065>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We consider a model of a random copolymer at a selective interface which undergoes a localization/delocalization transition. In spite of the several rigorous results available for this model, the theoretical characterization of the phase transition has remained elusive and there is still no agreement about several important issues, for example the behavior of the polymer near the phase transition line. From a rigorous viewpoint non coinciding upper and lower bounds on the critical line are known. In this paper we combine numerical computations with rigorous arguments to get to a better understanding of the phase diagram. Our main results include: - Various numerical observations that suggest that the critical line lies strictly in between the two bounds. - A rigorous statistical test based on concentration inequalities and super-additivity, for determining whether a given point of the phase diagram is in the localized phase. This is applied in particular to show that, with a very low level of error, the lower bound does not coincide with the critical line. - An analysis of the precise asymptotic behavior of the partition function in the delocalized phase, with particular attention to the effect of rare atypical stretches in the disorder sequence and on whether or not in the delocalized regime the polymer path has a Brownian scaling. - A new proof of the lower bound on the critical line. This proof relies on a characterization of the localized regime which is more appealing for interpreting the numerical data.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We consider a model of a random copolymer at a selective interface which undergoes a localization/delocalization transition. In spite of the several rigorous results available for this model, the theoretical characterization of the phase transition has remained elusive and there is still no agreement about several important issues, for example the behavior of the polymer near the phase transition line. From a rigorous viewpoint non coinciding upper and lower bounds on the critical line are known. In this paper we combine numerical computations with rigorous arguments to get to a better understanding of the phase diagram. Our main results include: - Various numerical observations that suggest that the critical line lies strictly in between the two bounds. - A rigorous statistical test based on concentration inequalities and super-additivity, for determining whether a given point of the phase diagram is in the localized phase. This is applied in particular to show that, with a very low level of error, the lower bound does not coincide with the critical line. - An analysis of the precise asymptotic behavior of the partition function in the delocalized phase, with particular attention to the effect of rare atypical stretches in the disorder sequence and on whether or not in the delocalized regime the polymer path has a Brownian scaling. - A new proof of the lower bound on the critical line. This proof relies on a characterization of the localized regime which is more appealing for interpreting the numerical data.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 28 September 2005; version 1 (arXiv v1), 28 September 2005<next-line>arXiv comments: accepted for publication on J. Stat. Phys<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|6420e8da711fc06a15f99af096789ca7...>|ocfl/copolymers-selective-interfaces/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Critical Behavior of the Two-Dimensional Randomly Driven Lattice Gas|paper/randomly-driven-lattice-gas/>>

    By Sergio Caracciolo, Andrea Gambassi, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Andrea Pelissetto.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/randomly-driven-lattice-gas/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/randomly-driven-lattice-gas/v1/content/source/RDLGf.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:cond-mat/0507614|https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0507614>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We investigate the critical behavior of the two-dimensional randomly driven lattice gas, in which particles are driven along one of the lattice axes by an infinite external field with randomly changing sign. A finite-size scaling (FSS) analysis provides novel evidences that this model is not in the same universality class as the driven lattice gas with a constant drive (DLG), contrarily to what has been recently reported in the literature. Indeed, the FSS functions of transverse observables (i.e., related to order-parameter fluctuations with wave vector perpendicular to the direction of the field) differ from the mean-field behavior predicted and observed within the DLG universality class. At variance with the DLG case, FSS is attained on lattices with fixed aspect ratio and anisotropy exponent equal to 1 and the transverse Binder cumulant does not vanish at the critical point.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We investigate the critical behavior of the two-dimensional randomly driven lattice gas, in which particles are driven along one of the lattice axes by an infinite external field with randomly changing sign. A finite-size scaling (FSS) analysis provides novel evidences that this model is not in the same universality class as the driven lattice gas with a constant drive (DLG), contrarily to what has been recently reported in the literature. Indeed, the FSS functions of transverse observables (i.e., related to order-parameter fluctuations with wave vector perpendicular to the direction of the field) differ from the mean-field behavior predicted and observed within the DLG universality class. At variance with the DLG case, FSS is attained on lattices with fixed aspect ratio and anisotropy exponent equal to 1 and the transverse Binder cumulant does not vanish at the critical point.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 26 July 2005; version 1 (arXiv v1), 26 July 2005<next-line>arXiv comments: 4 pages, 4 figures<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|9e05757fa1352559d47d74b30ece96d5...>|ocfl/randomly-driven-lattice-gas/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Remarks on the K41 scaling law in turbulent fluids|paper/k41-scaling-law/>>

    By F. Flandoli, M. Gubinelli, M. Hairer and M. Romito.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/k41-scaling-law/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/k41-scaling-law/v1/content/source/k41scaling.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math-ph/0507044|https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0507044>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        A definition of K41 scaling law for suitable families of measures is given and investigated. First, a number of necessary conditions are proved. They imply the absence of scaling laws for 2D stochastic Navier-Stokes equations and for the stochastic Stokes (linear) problem in any dimension, while they imply a lower bound on the mean vortex stretching in 3D. Second, for 3D stochastic Navier-Stokes equations necessary and sufficient conditions for K41 are proved, translating the problem into bounds for energy and enstrophy of high and low modes respectively. The validity of such conditions in 3D remains open. Finally, a stochastic vortex model with such properties is presented.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        A definition of K41 scaling law for suitable families of measures is given and investigated. First, a number of necessary conditions are proved. They imply the absence of scaling laws for 2D stochastic Navier-Stokes equations and for the stochastic Stokes (linear) problem in any dimension, while they imply a lower bound on the mean vortex stretching in 3D. Second, for 3D stochastic Navier-Stokes equations necessary and sufficient conditions for K41 are proved, translating the problem into bounds for energy and enstrophy of high and low modes respectively. The validity of such conditions in 3D remains open. Finally, a stochastic vortex model with such properties is presented.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 18 July 2005; version 1 (arXiv v1), 18 July 2005<next-line>arXiv comments: 40 pages, 2 figures<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|5bfae4d8c4c966393351d3f20b5dc2df...>|ocfl/k41-scaling-law/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Young integrals and SPDEs|paper/young-integrals-spdes/>>

    By Antoine Lejay, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Samy Tindel.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/young-integrals-spdes/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/young-integrals-spdes/v1/content/source/math-0407294.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math/0407294|https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0407294>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        In this note, we study the non-linear evolution problem <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|dY_t = -A Y_t dt + B(Y_t) dX_t>><specific|html*|\\(dY_t = -A Y_t dt + B(Y_t) dX_t\\)>, where <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|X>><specific|html*|\\(X\\)> is a <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)>-Hölder continuous function of the time parameter, with values in a distribution space, and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|-A>><specific|html*|\\(-A\\)> the generator of an analytical semigroup. Then, we will give some sharp conditions on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|X>><specific|html*|\\(X\\)> in order to solve the above equation in a function space, first in the linear case (for any value of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)> in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|(0,1)>><specific|html*|\\((0,1)\\)>), and then when <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|B>><specific|html*|\\(B\\)> satisfies some Lipschitz type conditions (for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma<gtr>1/2>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma<gtr>1/2\\)>). The solution of the evolution problem will be understood in the mild sense, and the integrals involved in that definition will be of Young type.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        In this note, we study the non-linear evolution problem <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|dY_t = -A Y_t dt + B(Y_t) dX_t>><specific|html*|\\(dY_t = -A Y_t dt + B(Y_t) dX_t\\)>, where <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|X>><specific|html*|\\(X\\)> is a <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)>-Hölder continuous function of the time parameter, with values in a distribution space, and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|-A>><specific|html*|\\(-A\\)> the generator of an analytical semigroup. Then, we will give some sharp conditions on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|X>><specific|html*|\\(X\\)> in order to solve the above equation in a function space, first in the linear case (for any value of <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)> in <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|(0,1)>><specific|html*|\\((0,1)\\)>), and then when <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|B>><specific|html*|\\(B\\)> satisfies some Lipschitz type conditions (for <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma<gtr>1/2>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma<gtr>1/2\\)>). The solution of the evolution problem will be understood in the mild sense, and the integrals involved in that definition will be of Young type.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 16 July 2004; version 1 (arXiv v1), 16 July 2004<next-line>arXiv comments: 22 pages, no figures<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|0775190dae8b002a5e6e61f286d97803...>|ocfl/young-integrals-spdes/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|The evolution of a random vortex filament|paper/random-vortex-filament/>>

    By Hakima Bessaih, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Francesco Russo.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/random-vortex-filament/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/random-vortex-filament/v2/content/source/math-0407141.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math/0407141|https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0407141>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We study an evolution problem in the space of continuous loops in three-dimensional Euclidean space modelled upon the dynamics of vortex lines in 3d incompressible and inviscid fluids. We establish existence of a local solution starting from Hölder regular loops with index greater than 1/3. When the Hölder regularity of the initial condition X is smaller or equal 1/2 we require X to be a rough path in the sense of Lyons. The solution will then live in an appropriate space of rough paths. In particular we can construct (local) solution starting from almost every Brownian loop.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We study an evolution problem in the space of continuous loops in three-dimensional Euclidean space modelled upon the dynamics of vortex lines in 3d incompressible and inviscid fluids. We establish existence of a local solution starting from Hölder regular loops with index greater than 1/3. When the Hölder regularity of the initial condition X is smaller or equal 1/2 we require X to be a rough path in the sense of Lyons. The solution will then live in an appropriate space of rough paths. In particular we can construct (local) solution starting from almost every Brownian loop.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 8 July 2004; version 2 (arXiv v2), 2 December 2004<next-line>arXiv comments: 25 pages, no figures, some additions, to appear in Ann. Probab<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|6b28263342b4d5ad61c3f85653e8efb4...>|ocfl/random-vortex-filament/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Statistics of a vortex filament model|paper/vortex-filament-statistics/>>

    By Franco Flandoli and Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/vortex-filament-statistics/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/vortex-filament-statistics/v1/content/source/math-0407076.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math/0407076|https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0407076>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        A random field composed by Poisson distributed Brownian vortex filaments is constructed. The filament have a random thickness, length and intensity, governed by a measure <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)>. Under appropriate assumptions on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)> we compute the scaling law of the structure function and get the multifractal scaling as a particular case.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        A random field composed by Poisson distributed Brownian vortex filaments is constructed. The filament have a random thickness, length and intensity, governed by a measure <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)>. Under appropriate assumptions on <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)> we compute the scaling law of the structure function and get the multifractal scaling as a particular case.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 6 July 2004; version 1 (arXiv v1), 6 July 2004<next-line>arXiv comments: 36 pages, no figures<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|b154718be46d9b954d62b3bf39767805...>|ocfl/vortex-filament-statistics/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Finite-Size Scaling in the Driven Lattice Gas|paper/driven-lattice-gas-scaling/>>

    By Sergio Caracciolo, Andrea Gambassi, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Andrea Pelissetto.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-scaling/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-scaling/v1/content/source/dlgJSP.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:cond-mat/0312175|https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0312175>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We present a Monte Carlo study of the high-temperature phase of the two-dimensional driven lattice gas at infinite driving field. We define a finite-volume correlation length, verify that this definition has a good infinite-volume limit independent of the lattice geometry, and study its finite-size-scaling behavior. The results for the correlation length are in good agreement with the predictions based on the field theory proposed by Janssen, Schmittmann, Leung, and Cardy. The theoretical predictions for the susceptibility and the magnetization are also well verified. We show that the transverse Binder parameter vanishes at the critical point in all dimensions <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d\\ge 2>><specific|html*|\\(d\\ge 2\\)> and discuss how such result should be expected in the theory of Janssen et al. in spite of the existence of a dangerously irrelevant operator. Our results confirm the Gaussian nature of the transverse excitations.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We present a Monte Carlo study of the high-temperature phase of the two-dimensional driven lattice gas at infinite driving field. We define a finite-volume correlation length, verify that this definition has a good infinite-volume limit independent of the lattice geometry, and study its finite-size-scaling behavior. The results for the correlation length are in good agreement with the predictions based on the field theory proposed by Janssen, Schmittmann, Leung, and Cardy. The theoretical predictions for the susceptibility and the magnetization are also well verified. We show that the transverse Binder parameter vanishes at the critical point in all dimensions <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|d\\ge 2>><specific|html*|\\(d\\ge 2\\)> and discuss how such result should be expected in the theory of Janssen et al. in spite of the existence of a dangerously irrelevant operator. Our results confirm the Gaussian nature of the transverse excitations.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 8 December 2003; version 1 (arXiv v1), 8 December 2003<next-line>arXiv comments: 40 pages, in honour of G. Jona-Lasinio<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|21a7527c2d75e88e7197c402727a325d...>|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-scaling/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Controlling Rough Paths|paper/controlling-rough-paths/>>

    By Massimiliano Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/controlling-rough-paths/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/controlling-rough-paths/v2/content/source/math-0306433.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math/0306433|https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0306433>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We formulate indefinite integration with respect to an irregular function as an algebraic problem and provide a criterion for the existence and uniqueness of a solution. This allows us to define a good notion of integral with respect to irregular paths with Hoelder exponent greater than 1/3 (e.g. samples of Brownian motion) and study the problem of the existence, uniqueness and continuity of solution of differential equations driven by such paths. We recover Young's theory of integration and the main results of Lyons' theory of rough paths in Hoelder topology.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We formulate indefinite integration with respect to an irregular function as an algebraic problem and provide a criterion for the existence and uniqueness of a solution. This allows us to define a good notion of integral with respect to irregular paths with Hoelder exponent greater than 1/3 (e.g. samples of Brownian motion) and study the problem of the existence, uniqueness and continuity of solution of differential equations driven by such paths. We recover Young's theory of integration and the main results of Lyons' theory of rough paths in Hoelder topology.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 30 June 2003; version 2 (arXiv v2), 28 October 2003<next-line>arXiv comments: 43 pages, no figures, corrected a proof in Sec. 6<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|436e907e543a1c68ab7ecf94ee6ccf97...>|ocfl/controlling-rough-paths/v2/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Comment on \<#201C\>Dynamic behavior of anisotropic non-equilibrium driving lattice gases\<#201D\>|paper/driven-lattice-gas-comment/>>

    By Sergio Caracciolo, Andrea Gambassi, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Andrea Pelissetto.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-comment/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-comment/v1/content/source/cond-mat-0305213.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:cond-mat/0305213|https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0305213>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        In a recent Letter Albano and Saracco study the dynamic critical behavior of some anisotropic driven lattice gases by Monte Carlo (MC) simulations. In this Comment we point out that the Ansätze they use to relate the measured scaling exponents with the critical exponents analytically computed within different field-theoretical approaches do not take properly into account the strongly anisotropic nature of the phase transition, by implicitly assuming <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|z = z_{\\bot} = z_{\\parallel}>><specific|html*|\\(z = z_{\\bot} = z_{\\parallel}\\)>. As a consequence, at variance with the claims by the authors, their MC data are not conclusive to determine which one of the field theories proposed in the literature correctly describes the universal properties of the phase transition in these lattice gases.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        In a recent Letter Albano and Saracco study the dynamic critical behavior of some anisotropic driven lattice gases by Monte Carlo (MC) simulations. In this Comment we point out that the Ansätze they use to relate the measured scaling exponents with the critical exponents analytically computed within different field-theoretical approaches do not take properly into account the strongly anisotropic nature of the phase transition, by implicitly assuming <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|z = z_{\\bot} = z_{\\parallel}>><specific|html*|\\(z = z_{\\bot} = z_{\\parallel}\\)>. As a consequence, at variance with the claims by the authors, their MC data are not conclusive to determine which one of the field theories proposed in the literature correctly describes the universal properties of the phase transition in these lattice gases.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 9 May 2003; version 1 (arXiv v1), 9 May 2003<next-line>arXiv comments: 1 page<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|140859b3ab423c8f9baff376cb7dd274...>|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-comment/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Shape dependence of the finite-size scaling limit in a strongly anisotropic <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|O(\\infty)>><specific|html*|\\(O(\\infty)\\)> model|paper/anisotropic-finite-size-shape/>>

    By Sergio Caracciolo, Andrea Gambassi, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Andrea Pelissetto.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/anisotropic-finite-size-shape/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/anisotropic-finite-size-shape/v1/content/source/shape6.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:cond-mat/0304297|https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0304297>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We discuss the shape dependence of the finite-size scaling limit in a strongly anisotropic O(N) model in the large-N limit. We show that scaling is observed even if an incorrect value for the anisotropy exponent is considered. However, the related exponents may only be effective ones, differing from the correct critical exponents of the model. We discuss the implications of our results for numerical finite-size scaling studies of strongly anisotropic systems.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We discuss the shape dependence of the finite-size scaling limit in a strongly anisotropic O(N) model in the large-N limit. We show that scaling is observed even if an incorrect value for the anisotropy exponent is considered. However, the related exponents may only be effective ones, differing from the correct critical exponents of the model. We discuss the implications of our results for numerical finite-size scaling studies of strongly anisotropic systems.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 12 April 2003; version 1 (arXiv v1), 12 April 2003<next-line>arXiv comments: 21 pages, 4 figures<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|3831411e29c153da3c77ca43e442b0a7...>|ocfl/anisotropic-finite-size-shape/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|On a relation between stochastic integration and geometric measure theory|paper/stochastic-currents/>>

    By Franco Flandoli, Mariano Giaquinta, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Vincenzo M. Tortorelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/stochastic-currents/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/stochastic-currents/v1/content/source/math-0211458.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math/0211458|https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0211458>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        Two problems are addressed for the path of certain stochastic processes: a) do they define currents? b) are these currents of a classical type? A general answer to question a) is given for processes like semimartingales or with Lyons-Zheng structure. As to question b), it is shown that Hölder continuous paths with exponent <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma <gtr> 1/2>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma <gtr> 1/2\\)> define integral flat chains.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        Two problems are addressed for the path of certain stochastic processes: a) do they define currents? b) are these currents of a classical type? A general answer to question a) is given for processes like semimartingales or with Lyons-Zheng structure. As to question b), it is shown that Hölder continuous paths with exponent <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma <gtr> 1/2>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma <gtr> 1/2\\)> define integral flat chains.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 29 November 2002; version 1 (arXiv v1), 29 November 2002<next-line>arXiv comments: 30 pages, no figures<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|0b4b48331a993afe76d4442ab3ad4aa9...>|ocfl/stochastic-currents/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Transverse Fluctuations in the Driven Lattice Gas|paper/driven-lattice-gas-fluctuations/>>

    By Sergio Caracciolo, Andrea Gambassi, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Andrea Pelissetto.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-fluctuations/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-fluctuations/v1/content/source/letter2.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:cond-mat/0211669|https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0211669>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We define a transverse correlation length suitable to discuss the finite-size-scaling behavior of an out-of-equilibrium lattice gas, whose correlation functions decay algebraically with the distance. By numerical simulations we verify that this definition has a good infinite-volume limit independent of the lattice geometry. We study the transverse fluctuations as they can select the correct field-theoretical description. By means of a careful finite-size scaling analysis, without tunable parameters, we show that they are Gaussian, in agreement with the predictions of the model proposed by Janssen, Schmittmann, Leung, and Cardy.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We define a transverse correlation length suitable to discuss the finite-size-scaling behavior of an out-of-equilibrium lattice gas, whose correlation functions decay algebraically with the distance. By numerical simulations we verify that this definition has a good infinite-volume limit independent of the lattice geometry. We study the transverse fluctuations as they can select the correct field-theoretical description. By means of a careful finite-size scaling analysis, without tunable parameters, we show that they are Gaussian, in agreement with the predictions of the model proposed by Janssen, Schmittmann, Leung, and Cardy.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 28 November 2002; version 1 (arXiv v1), 28 November 2002<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|39e99f62f5bd4f31bd4f47ff82710722...>|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-fluctuations/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Finite-Size Critical Behavior of the Driven Lattice Gas|paper/driven-lattice-gas-finite-size/>>

    By Sergio Caracciolo, Andrea Gambassi, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Andrea Pelissetto.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-finite-size/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-finite-size/v1/content/source/DLG.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:cond-mat/0106221|https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0106221>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We present a detailed Monte Carlo study of the high-temperature phase of the two-dimensional driven lattice gas at infinite driving field. We define a finite-volume correlation length, study its finite-size-scaling behavior and extrapolate it to the infinite-volume limit by using an iterative extrapolation method. The same method is applied to the susceptibility. We determine the transverse exponents <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)> and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\nu>><specific|html*|\\(\\nu\\)>. They turn out to be in perfect agreement with the theoretical predictions. We also compute the transverse Binder parameter, which apparently vanishes at the critical point, and the distribution of the magnetization. These results confirm the Gaussian nature of the transverse excitations.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We present a detailed Monte Carlo study of the high-temperature phase of the two-dimensional driven lattice gas at infinite driving field. We define a finite-volume correlation length, study its finite-size-scaling behavior and extrapolate it to the infinite-volume limit by using an iterative extrapolation method. The same method is applied to the susceptibility. We determine the transverse exponents <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\gamma>><specific|html*|\\(\\gamma\\)> and <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\nu>><specific|html*|\\(\\nu\\)>. They turn out to be in perfect agreement with the theoretical predictions. We also compute the transverse Binder parameter, which apparently vanishes at the critical point, and the distribution of the magnetization. These results confirm the Gaussian nature of the transverse excitations.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 12 June 2001; version 1 (arXiv v1), 12 June 2001<next-line>arXiv comments: laTeX2e, 38 pages, 13 eps figures<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|5568f5c784a003ca05783987634d0c59...>|ocfl/driven-lattice-gas-finite-size/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|Finite-Size Correlation Length and Violations of Finite-Size Scaling|paper/finite-size-scaling-violations/>>

    By Sergio Caracciolo, Andrea Gambassi, Massimiliano Gubinelli and Andrea Pelissetto.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/finite-size-scaling-violations/v1/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/finite-size-scaling-violations/v1/content/source/cond-mat-0010479.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:cond-mat/0010479|https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0010479>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We address the problem of the definition of the finite-volume correlation length. First, we study the large-N limit of the N-vector model, and we show the existence of several constraints on the definition if regularity of the finite-size scaling functions and correct anomalous behaviour above the upper critical dimension are required. Then, we study in detail a model in which the zero mode in prohibited. Such a model is a generalization of the fixed-magnetization Ising model which is equivalent to the lattice gas. Also in this case, we find that the finite-volume correlation length must satisfy appropriate constraints in order to obtain regular finite-size scaling functions, and, above the upper critical dimension, an anomalous scaling behaviour. The large-N results are confirmed by a one-loop calculation in the lattice <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\phi^4>><specific|html*|\\(\\phi^4\\)> theory.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We address the problem of the definition of the finite-volume correlation length. First, we study the large-N limit of the N-vector model, and we show the existence of several constraints on the definition if regularity of the finite-size scaling functions and correct anomalous behaviour above the upper critical dimension are required. Then, we study in detail a model in which the zero mode in prohibited. Such a model is a generalization of the fixed-magnetization Ising model which is equivalent to the lattice gas. Also in this case, we find that the finite-volume correlation length must satisfy appropriate constraints in order to obtain regular finite-size scaling functions, and, above the upper critical dimension, an anomalous scaling behaviour. The large-N results are confirmed by a one-loop calculation in the lattice <specific|texmacs|<verbatim|\\phi^4>><specific|html*|\\(\\phi^4\\)> theory.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 30 October 2000; version 1 (arXiv v1), 30 October 2000<next-line>arXiv comments: 19 pages, Latex2e<next-line>pin:
    <hlink|<verbatim|f199a9808ac52515005a3f0f4c66eec1...>|ocfl/finite-size-scaling-violations/v1/inventory.json>>
  </notes-news-item>

  <\notes-news-item>
    <with|font-series|bold|<hlink|The Gibbs ensemble of a vortex filament|paper/vortex-filament-gibbs-ensemble/>>

    By F. Flandoli and M. Gubinelli.

    <hlink|PDF|ocfl/vortex-filament-gibbs-ensemble/v2/content/paper.pdf> \<cdot\> <hlink|LaTeX source|ocfl/vortex-filament-gibbs-ensemble/v2/content/source/math-ph-0009005.tex> \<cdot\> <hlink|arXiv:math-ph/0009005|https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0009005>

    <specific|texmacs|<\folded>
      Abstract
    <|folded>
        We introduce a statistical ensemble for a single vortex filament of a three dimensional incompressible fluid. The core of the vortex is modelled by a quite generic stochastic process. We prove the existence of the partition function for both positive and a limited range of negative temperatures.
    </folded>><specific|html*|<html-tag|details|<html-tag|summary|Abstract><\html-div-class|paper-abstract>
        We introduce a statistical ensemble for a single vortex filament of a three dimensional incompressible fluid. The core of the vortex is modelled by a quite generic stochastic process. We prove the existence of the partition function for both positive and a limited range of negative temperatures.
    </html-div-class>>>

    <with|font-size|0.84|par-mode|left|first issued 5 September 2000; version 2 (arXiv v2), 1 November 2000<next-line>arXiv comments: 32 pages, submitted to Prob. Th. and Rel. Fields, minor typo corrections<next-line>pin:
